4. The amount of the assessment
4.1 The amount of the assessment under the self-assessment rules
Subsection 155-15(3) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides for 2 ways to work out the amount of the assessment.
Paragraph 155-15(3)(a) provides that the assessed amount is the amount stated in the relevant document (listed in the table in subsection 155-15(1)) if that document is required to state the assessable amount. This includes a nil amount.
Otherwise, paragraph 155-15(3)(b) provides that the assessed amount is worked out based on the information contained in the document. This includes a nil amount.
 Example 10: Using a GST return to calculate the assessed amount
Ale runs a furniture shop and is registered for GST.
On 28 October, Ale lodged their GST return for the tax period ending 30 September. The GST return showed an amount of $10,458 at label 1A (GST payable) and $6,358 at 1B (input tax credits).
Ale's assessed amount (net amount) is worked out in based on the information in the GST return by deducting input tax credits from GST payable.
Ale’s net amount is $4,100.
4.2 The amount of the assessment of a taxable importation
Subsection 155-20(3) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the assessed amount is the amount worked out based on the information contained in the 2 documents listed in the table in subsection 155-20(1). This includes a nil amount.
