7. Amendments after the period of review
7.1 Making an amendment after the period of review
There are exceptions that allow the Commissioner to amend an assessment after the period of review has ended.
If all the relevant requirements have been met, the Commissioner may make an amendment after the period of review:
•	if you have applied for an amendment (section 155-45 of Schedule 1 to the TAA)
•	to give effect to a private ruling (section 155-50)
•	to give effect to certain anti-avoidance declarations (section 155-55)
•	to give effect to a decision resulting from a review or objection (section 155-60), or
•	the Commissioner considers there has been fraud or evasion (section 155-60).
Where an amendment is made in any of the above situations, the Commissioner must give the entity a notice of amended assessment.
The amendment does not give rise to a refreshed period of review.
 More information
For more information, see ‘Refreshed periods of review’ in this product.
However, the entity retains the right to object to the amended assessment in accordance with Pt IVC of the TAA.
7.2 Applying for an amendment
Section 155-45 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment of yours at any time if you apply for an amendment (in the approved form) during the period of review.
The Commissioner may amend the assessment to give effect to the decision on the application.
 Example 16: Written request of an amendment
Angelo is registered for GST and lodges their 31 July 2019 monthly activity statement on 21 August 2019.
On 2 August 2023 Angelo writes to the Commissioner (in the approved form) seeking an amendment to that assessment.
The Commissioner amends Angelo's assessment as requested on 5 September 2023.
This is allowed as Angelo applied for the amendment within the period of review.
Section 155-45 or 155-40
The way in which a taxpayer requests an amendment and the decision the Commissioner makes on that request will determine whether section 155-45 or section 155-40 of Schedule 1 to the TAA applies to that request.
Section 155-45 will apply where application for an amendment is made:
•	in writing (not using a GST return)
•	during the period of review but the alteration is not fully accepted, or
•	during the period of review, but the amendment is made after the period of review.
7.3 Giving effect to a private ruling
Section 155-50 of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment of yours at any time if:
•	you apply for a private ruling during the period of review for the assessment, and
•	the Commissioner makes a private ruling because of the application.
The Commissioner may amend the assessment to give effect to the ruling.
A private ruling is an expression of the Commissioner's opinion of the way in which a relevant provision applies (or would apply) to an entity in relation to a specified scheme.
Section 155-50 ensures that the Commissioner is not prevented from amending an assessment simply because the amendment was dependent upon a private ruling that had not been decided within the period of review.
 
 Example 17: Giving effect to a private ruling
Ananda is registered for GST and lodges their 31 March 2020 activity statement on 28 April 2020.
On 15 February 2024, Ananda applies for a private ruling in relation to that assessment. The Commissioner issues the ruling to Ananda on 1 May 2024.
The Commissioner may amend Ananda’s assessment for the tax period ending 31 March 2020 to give effect to the ruling, even though the period of review ended on 29 April 2024.
7.4 Giving effect to certain anti-avoidance declarations
Paragraph 155-55(a) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment if the Commissioner makes a declaration under subsection 165-45(3) of the GST Act (about compensating adjustments for anti-avoidance declarations).
Under the subsection 165-45(3), the Commissioner can make a declaration compensating an entity other than the avoider for a GST disadvantage that the entity has got from a scheme.
The Commissioner may amend the assessment to give effect to the declaration.
Where the Commissioner makes an amendment to give effect to a GST anti-avoidance declaration, they must issue a notice of amended assessment as soon as practical after the assessment is made.
Taxpayers dissatisfied with the Commissioner’s amended assessment can lodge an objection in accordance with Part IVC of the TAA.
7.5 Reviews and objections
Paragraph 155-60(a) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment of yours at any time to give effect to a decision on a review or appeal.
Paragraph 155-60(b) provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment of yours at any time:
•	as a result of an objection made by you, or
•	pending a review or appeal.
Where the Commissioner makes an amendment because of a review or an objection they must issue a notice of amended assessment as soon as practical after the assessment is made.
Taxpayers dissatisfied with the Commissioner's amended assessment can lodge an objection in accordance with Part IVC of the TAA.
 Example 18: Objection
Aleki is registered for GST. On 28 October 2019 they lodge a GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2019.
On 17 September 2023, the Commissioner amends Aleki’s assessment and increases the net amount by $1,000.
On 18 October 2023, Aleki objects to the amended assessment under Part IVC of the TAA.
The Commissioner reviews their decision and allows the objection on 30 October 2023.
The Commissioner may amend Aleki's assessment to give effect to the Commissioner’s decision on the objection, even though the period of review for the assessment ended on 29 October 2023.
 Example 19: Review or repeal
Angela is registered for GST. On 28 October 2019 they lodge a GST return for the tax period ending 30 September 2019.
On 17 September 2023, the Commissioner amends Angela’s assessment and increases the net amount by $1,000.
On 18 October 2023, Angela objects to the amended assessment under Part IVC of the TAA.
The Commissioner reviews their decision and disallows the objection on 30 October 2023.
If Angela appeals to the Administrative Appeals Tribunal (AAT) or the Federal Court, the Commissioner can amend the assessment to give effect to the decision of the Tribunal or Court. The power to amend extends to giving effect to the results of any appeals to a higher judicial body.
 
7.6 Fraud or evasion
Paragraph 155-60(c) of Schedule 1 to the TAA provides that the Commissioner may amend an assessment of yours at any time if they are of the opinion there has been fraud or evasion.
 More information
For more information, see Practice Statement Law Administration PS LA 2008/6 Fraud or evasion.
Fraud can be described as the making of a statement (in this context relevant to the entity's liability to GST) which the maker:
•	knows or believes to be false, or
•	is recklessly careless whether it is true or false.
Evasion contemplates some blameworthy act or omission.
Whether there is fraud or evasion is based on the facts and circumstances existing at the time of the alleged fraud or evasion (for example, when the GST return was lodged).
The fraud or evasion need not be committed by the entity personally. The unlimited amendment period may also apply where fraud or evasion is committed by someone else on the entity's behalf (with or without the entity's knowledge). For example, if it is committed by a tax agent when preparing the GST return.
In both AAT and Federal Court proceedings, the taxpayer has the burden of proof. In other words, the taxpayer must prove there has not been fraud or evasion. In such cases, the taxpayer must prove on the balance of probabilities there was no fraud or evasion, rather than proving it beyond reasonable doubt.
