2.	TAXIS
Division 144 of the GST Act contains the special rules for taxis.
2.1	REQUIREMENT TO REGISTER
Section 144-5 of the GST Act provides that an entity which provides taxi travel is required to register for GST. 
As you have read an entity that provides taxi travel is required to register regardless of:
•	its GST turnover
•	whether it makes other types of supplies in addition to taxi travel.
 
Example – requirement to register
John has an ABN and carries on two enterprises. During the day he supplies taxi travel with a GST turnover of $30,000. During the evenings John performs as an entertainer with a GST turnover of $35,000. His combined GST turnover is less than the $75,000 registration threshold.
However, because he supplies taxi travel, he must register irrespective of his turnover. 
2.2	WHY ARE SUPPLIERS OF TAXI TRAVEL REQUIRED TO REGISTER
The Taxi industry – issues register on the ATO website explains why suppliers of taxi travel are required to register for GST.
It states that the government chose to apply compulsory registration of taxi drivers for several reasons including the following.
•	To avoid the confusion that would be created if some taxis had to charge GST and others did not. 
•	To avoid the added problem that would arise if a passenger was using a taxi for a business trip (creditable acquisition). In this instance, the passenger would want to be able to claim an input tax credit for the GST included in the fare. 
•	Meter rates are set by each state authority and after 1 July 2000 all meters were adjusted to reflect the GST. If the ordinary registration turnover threshold were to apply, some drivers would be registered and others would not, even though all would be collecting at the higher rate. This would disadvantage drivers who had to be registered.
2.3	WHAT IS TAXI TRAVEL?
As an entity that supplies taxi travel is required to register for GST, it is important to know what is meant by a supply of taxi travel and which supplies fall outside of the definition.
Available for public hire v private hire
Taxi travel is defined in section 195-1 of the GST Act.
 	Read the definition of ‘taxi travel’ in section 195-1 of the GST Act.
There have been a number of ATO interpretative decisions which show the Commissioner's interpretation of the meaning of taxi travel.
ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2002/23 Goods and Services Tax GST and registration of a wedding car operator provides that the terms 'taxi' and 'limousine' are not defined in the GST Act and therefore take on their ordinary meaning. The Macquarie Dictionary defines 'taxi' as, 'a motor car for public hire, especially one fitted with a taximeter'.
In addition, the Macquarie Dictionary defines 'limousine' as any large luxurious car. Some, if not all, of the vehicles that the entity (from ATO ID 2002/23) uses would satisfy the ordinary definition of a limousine. However, the definition of 'limousine' should be interpreted in accordance with the context in which the term 'limousine' is used. 
Section 144-1 of the GST Act provides that taxi operators are required to register for GST regardless of turnover. The term 'limousine' is therefore used in the context of taxi operations. Taking this into account, it is considered that only limousine services that are provided by the taxi industry or are similar to and essentially in competition with the taxi industry are 'taxi travel'. 
To fall within the definition of taxi travel, taxis and limousines must be available for public hire.
This interpretation is supported by ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2002/24 Goods and Services Tax GST and reception and transfer services.
This would therefore exclude vehicles used only for private hire such as for weddings, or fixed-price reception and transfer services for overseas students.
Taxi drivers who are employees only
The ATO view given in the Taxi industry – issues register is that a person who is only employed as a driver at a set rate and is not entitled to keep any of the takings, is not supplying taxi travel and is not required to register. It is the employer who is the supplier of taxi travel.
 
Example – taxi driver who is an employee
Judd is employed by ABC Taxis. ABC Taxis pays Judd at a fixed hourly rate to drive their taxis. Judd does not get a share of the fare.
Judd is not providing taxi travel.
ABC Taxis is providing taxi travel and is required to register for GST regardless of its GST turnover. 
Taxi drivers under a bailment arrangement
The Taxi industry – issues register provides that most drivers operate under a bailment arrangement. This means that a driver makes a payment of some kind that allows them to use the taxi to drive. The payment could be in the form of lease payments or an amount that is paid at the end of each shift. It does not matter whether the amount is a set amount or a percentage of the shift takings. The driver shares in the takings from the hire of the taxi and is not an employee and will be required to register as a supplier of taxi travel.
 
Example – bailment arrangement
Lee is a taxi driver. Lee leases a taxi licence and cab from TaxiCo. Lee pays TaxiCo 50% of his takings and keeps the remainder for himself.
Lee is providing taxi travel and is required to register regardless of his GST turnover. 
Leasing a taxi plate and cabs
The Taxi industry – issues register provides that owners or bailors who also drive some shifts are also required to register for GST regardless of their turnover. Owners or bailors who do not drive shifts and just lease out the license and cab are not supplying taxi travel and have the choice of whether or not to register if their turnover is under $75,000. Like all other businesses, owners must register if their turnover exceeds $75,000.
This is how the Commissioner interprets the law in ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2002/486 Goods and Services Tax GST and taxi plate owner that does not provide taxi travel.
 
Example – leasing a taxi licence
Gina works full time as a bank manager. She thought that investing in a taxi licence and leasing it would be a good investment so she has done so. The taxi driver who uses the licence pays Gina a set amount regardless of their takings and Gina does not drive the taxi herself.
Gina is not providing taxi travel and is not required to register for GST unless her GST turnover meets the registration turnover threshold. 
Motor cars v other vehicles
The ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2004/478 Goods and Services Tax GST and registration of a cycle rickshaw operator and ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2004/523 Goods and Services Tax GST and registration of motor powered tricycle operator examine whether a supply of transport using such vehicles is a supply of taxi travel.
Both rely on the definitions of taxi and of limousine in the Macquarie Dictionary to exclude such supplies from the operation of Division 144 of the GST Act.
 
Exercise 1
Which of the following entities must register for GST under section 144-5 of the GST Act?
a. A business that privately hires their vehicle out for weddings under a restricted licence.
b. A supplier of taxi travel with a GST turnover of $50,000.
c. A taxi driver who is paid an hourly rate as an employee of a taxi company.
d. A taxi plate owner who leases the plate to a taxi company and does not drive the taxi themselves.
Solution
b
a. For section 144-5 of the GST Act to apply to an entity it must be making a supply of taxi travel. Under section 195-1 of the GST Act taxi travel means 'travel that involves transporting passengers, by taxi or limousine, for fares’. ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2002/23 Goods and Services Tax GST and registration of a wedding car operator provides that the terms 'taxi' and 'limousine' are not defined in the GST Act and therefore take on their ordinary meaning. The Macquarie Dictionary defines 'taxi' as, 'a motor car for public hire, especially one fitted with a taximeter'.
An entity that privately hires their vehicle out for weddings is not providing a motor car for public hire and is therefore not making a supply of taxi travel.
b. The facts state that the entity is making a supply of taxi travel. Under section 144-5 of the GST Act an entity that makes a supply of taxi travel is required to register for GST regardless of its GST turnover.
c. The taxi driver is paid at an hourly rate and would be regarded as an employee providing his employee services. The driver is not making a supply of taxi travel (and is in fact not carrying on an enterprise). This view is given in the Taxi industry – issues register.
d. A taxi plate owner who leases the plate to a taxi company and does not drive a taxi themselves is not making a supply of taxi travel. This view is given in the Taxi industry – issues register. It is also given in ATO Interpretative Decision ATO ID 2002/486 Goods and Services Tax GST and taxi plate owner that does not provide taxi travel.
