3.	LIMITED REGISTRATION ENTITIES
Division 146 of the GST Act contains the special rules for limited registration entities.
3.1	WHO IS ELIGIBLE TO BE A LIMITED REGISTRATION ENTITY?
Limited registration entities came about as a consequence or laws that brought offshore supplies of intangibles and low value goods into the GST system.
Subsection 146-5(2) of the GST Act describes which entities are eligible to become a limited registration entity.
Elections, revocations and cancellations
Subsection 146-5(1) provides that an entity is a limited registration entity as long as its election to be one is in effect.
Subsections 146-5(3) to (6) cover; dates of effect, revocations and cancellations.
3.2	LIMITED REGISTRATION ENTITIES CANNOT MAKE CREDITABLE ACQUISITIONS OR CREDITABLE IMPORTATIONS
There are fewer checks and compliance requirements placed on a limited registration entity. As a result there are also a number of restrictions that apply to such entities.
Under subsection 146-10(1) of the GST Act, a limited registration entity cannot make a creditable acquisition.
However under subsection 146-10(2), if that entity becomes ‘fully registered’ within the time period provided for, the entity will be entitled to treat its acquisitions as creditable acquisitions if all the usual requirements are met.
Section 146-15 provides for similar rules in relation to creditable importations.
3.3	NO ABR ENTRY
Under section 146-20 of the GST Act, the date of GST registration for a limited registration entity is not entered in the Australian Business Register.
This is as a consequence of subsection 8(3) of the A New Tax System (Australian Business Number) Act 1999 (ABN Act) which states that a limited registration entity is not entitled to an ABN.
As a limited registration entity is not entitled to an ABN, the Registrar is unable to enter the details required under section 25 of the ABN Act. Therefore, limited registration entities are not entered in the ABR.
3.4	QUARTERLY TAX PERIODS
Under section 146-25 of the GST Act, limited registration entities can only have quarterly tax periods.
4.	GOVERNMENT ENTITIES
Division 149 of the GST Act contains the special rules for government entities. 
4.1	GOVERNMENT ENTITIES MAY REGISTER
Section 149-5 of the GST Act provides that a government entity may register for GST even if it is not an entity and not carrying on an enterprise.
This special rule prevents the GST becoming a burden upon a government entity. Should the entity have not been able to register for GST, it would not be able to reclaim the GST incurred on acquisitions as an input tax credit, increasing the cost of the activities undertaken.
Section 149-1 of the GST Act explains that parts of the Commonwealth, a State or a Territory may register for GST even if they are not separate legal entities.
Government entity is defined in section 195-1 of the GST Act by referring to the definition in section 41 of the A New Tax System (Australian Business Number) Act 1999 (ABN Act). 
The Explanatory Memorandum to the A New Tax System (Indirect Tax and Consequential Amendments) Bill (No. 2) 1999 explains that without using the definition in the ABN Act that all government organisations would effectively be part of a single State, Territory and Commonwealth registration and each Government would decide which sub-entities it would treat as separate branches for GST purposes. The ABN Act includes a definition of 'government entity' which allows for separate registration of government entities at a lower level.
A government entity may choose to be separately registered for a number of reasons.
The entity may have a separate accounting system that makes it difficult to be included as part of another entity's GST registration. Alternatively, the entity may have separate activities or be located in a distant geographical location that makes a single GST registration unworkable.
The legislation does not prescribe the situations in which the decision must be applied. Therefore, it can be used in any instance to ease the administrative burden associated with accounting for GST on supplies made by government entities. 
4.2	GOVERNMENT ENTITIES ARE NOT REQUIRED TO BE REGISTERED
While section 149-5 of the GST Act allows a government entity to register for GST even if it is not carrying on an enterprise, section 149-10 of the GST Act removes the requirement of the entity to register.
In practice this section may have little effect since the intergovernmental agreement requires all government agencies to register for GST.
4.3	GOVERNMENT ENTITIES ARE NOT REQUIRED TO CANCEL THEIR REGISTRATION
Section 149-20 of the GST Act provides that government entities are not required to cancel their registration.
Sections 149-5, 149-10 and 149-20 of the GST Act combine to give government entities total flexibility in relation to GST registration.
4.4	THE GST ACT APPLIES TO REGISTERED GOVERNMENT ENTITIES
Section 149-15 of the GST Act provides that the GST Act applies to a registered government entity.
Where a government entity is separately registered, it is treated as if it is a separate legal entity carrying on an enterprise. Accordingly, it is required to pay GST on all its taxable supplies and is entitled to input tax credits on creditable acquisitions, and so on. This includes transaction with other government entities.
4.5	MEMBERSHIP REQUIREMENTS OF A GOVERNMENT GST GROUP
Section 149-25 of the GST Act describes the membership requirements of a GST group of government related entities.
The Explanatory Memorandum to the A New Tax System (Indirect Tax and Consequential Amendments) Bill (No. 2) 1999 explains that section 149-25 of the GST Act applies to a government entity and a government-related entity. A government related entity includes an entity that would be a government entity but for subparagraph (e)(i) of the definition of government entity in the ABN Act.
 
Exercise 2
Which of the following statements is true?
a. Government entities that do not carry on an enterprise cannot register for GST.
b. The Commissioner must cancel the registration of a government entity if it is not likely to be carrying on an enterprise for at least 12 months.
c. A government department can be a member of a GST group.
Solution
c
a. Section 149-5 of the GST Act provides that government entities may apply to be registered for GST even if they are not carrying on an enterprise. In addition the Commissioner must register such entities.
b. Under section 149-20 a government entity is not required to cancel their registration even if they are not carrying on an enterprise.
c. Section 149-25 of the GST Act describes the membership requirements for a government related entity for a GST group. If the requirements are satisfied the entity can become a member of a GST group under Division 48 of the GST Act.
