Record keeping
Good record keeping supports:
•	information being able to be found when it is needed
•	creation of full and accurate records at any point in time
•	promotion of information sharing and collaboration
•	confidence in the information and provides authenticity and integrity
•	past decisions and actions being understood and that the information will withstand scrutiny as evidence.
Record keeping is important as it results in information that can be trusted and data that is in context, supporting our business decisions. Good record keeping shows that decisions have been considered and appropriate actions taken and this is critical when decisions are questioned or challenged.

 
Client Engagement
Client engagement underpins a client’s perceptions of our organisation and refers to the connection between the client and the ATO.
Effective client engagement is reflected in:
•	our interactions with clients during the course of our engagements and how these interactions shape their experience
•	actively working with the client to achieve the case outcome in a timely manner
•	ensuring the client has understood the decisions that have been made and education has been provided to support future compliance
•	proactive communication throughout the case
•	keeping the client informed of what is happening and when, as well as what is expected of them.
The importance of building a positive working relationship with clients and keeping them informed is pivotal to client satisfaction and maintaining the client’s trust and confidence. A good client experience supports continued engagement in the tax system and client retention.

  
Timeliness
Irrespective of cycle time, a case or work item is timely if it is:
•	resolved without unnecessary delay
•	any delays were mitigated to the degree an ordinary person would find acceptable
•	actively managed using all tools and resources available.
 
Timeliness is important to clients, but it cannot be the sole driver of a decision if it leads to quality being sacrificed and incomplete decisions being made.
 
High Client Impact
High Client Impact means the circumstances or actions are likely to strongly affect someone or something. These actions can still be appropriate, but you must consider the client’s situation and the effect of your decision.
Sometimes, a series of decisions or actions can have an unintended impact on the client. This may include financial, emotional or reputational costs, as well as uncertainty caused by delays.
We need safeguards that identify these circumstances and actions. This helps staff make informed decisions and supports a positive client experience.
 
