Course description
This course describes when a supply is a taxable supply and how you calculate the GST payable.
Course details
•	Duration: 1 hour and 30 minutes
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: All ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: None
Learning outcomes
At the end of this course, you will be able to:
•	Determine if a supply is a taxable supply.
•	Calculate the GST payable on a taxable supply. 
1. Taxable supplies
Section 9-5 of the A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (GST Act) provides that you make a taxable supply if all the following requirements are met.
•	You make the supply for consideration.
•	The supply is made in the course or furtherance of an enterprise that you carry on.
•	The supply is connected with the indirect tax zone.
•	You are registered, or required to be registered, for GST.
However, the supply is not a taxable supply to the extent that it is either GST-free or input taxed.
 Attention
There are special rules that:
•	make a supply taxable even if all the requirements in section 9-5 are not met
•	make a supply non-taxable even if all the requirements in section 9-5 are met.
We briefly outline some of these in the ‘special rules’ chapter in this product. 
 Reflection question 1: taxable supply – important concepts
You have seen the definition of a taxable supply.
What do you think are the important concepts in the definition?
Answer
•	you make
•	supply
•	consideration, and for consideration
•	in the course or furtherance of an enterprise
•	enterprise
•	enterprise that you carry on
•	indirect tax zone
•	connected with the indirect tax zone
•	registered for GST
•	required to be registered for GST
•	to the extent that the supply is GST-free or input taxed
•	GST-free and input taxed.

 Example 1: taxable and non-taxable supplies
Ron began a new business in Brisbane on 1 July last year. Ron’s business is selling travel and camping goods including tents, clothing, luggage, equipment and footwear. Ron was required to register for GST as of 1 January this year and did so.
Not a taxable supply
In November last year Ron sold a backpack to a customer for $220. Ron’s sale was not a taxable supply. Ron was not registered for GST, nor required to be registered. Therefore, the sale did not meet all the requirements for a taxable supply.
A taxable supply
In January, Ron sold a pair of hiking shoes to a customer for $99. Ron’s sale is a taxable supply as:
•	Ron sold the shoes
•	Ron received $99 for the shoes
•	Ron sold the shoes as part of their business
•	Ron sold the shoes in Brisbane
•	Ron was registered for GST
•	Ron’s sale was neither GST-free nor input taxed.
1.1 GST-free and input taxed supplies
The GST Act defines which supplies are GST-free, and which supplies are input taxed.
Division 38 lists supplies that are GST-free and describes when they are GST-free. Examples of GST-free supplies include:
•	food
•	education
•	exports
•	activities of charities
•	going concerns
•	cars for the disabled.
 
Division 40 lists supplies that are input taxed and describes when they are input taxed. Examples of input taxed supplies include:
•	financial supplies
•	residential premises
•	school tuckshops and canteens.
 Reflection question 2: use of the term ‘extent’
Section 9-5 of the GST Act provides that a supply is not taxable to the extent that it is GST-free or input taxed.
What do you think the implication of the term is?
Answer
It means that a supply can be partly taxable and partly GST-free or input taxed.
