3. Consideration
For a supply to be taxable under section 9-5 of the GST Act, it must be made for consideration. The definition of consideration is intended to be very broad. It extends beyond payments.
Subsection 9-15(1) of the GST Act provides that consideration includes:
•	any payment, act or forbearance in connection with a supply of anything
•	any payment, act or forbearance in response to, or for, inducing a supply of anything.
Paragraph 102 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2012/2 Goods and services tax: financial assistance payments explains that the definition is intended to be broader than it is for contractual purposes.
Paragraph 12 of Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2001/6 Goods and services tax: non-monetary consideration explains that our view is that payment is not limited to a payment of money. It includes a payment in a non-monetary form such as providing goods.
Subsections 9-15(2) and (2A) provide that it does not matter if:
•	the payment, act or forbearance was voluntary or not
•	the payment, act or forbearance was by the recipient or not
•	the payment, act or forbearance was in compliance with an order of a court or similar body
•	the payment, act or forbearance was in compliance with a settlement relating to proceedings before a court or similar body.
 Example 4: non-monetary payment
ABC Pty Ltd swaps a $1,100 photocopier for a $1,100 facsimile with XYZ Pty Ltd.
The facsimile is consideration for ABC Pty Ltd’s supply of the photocopier, and the photocopier is consideration for XYZ Pty Ltd’s supply of the facsimile.
Subsection 9-15(2B) of the GST Act provides that a payment, act or forbearance is consideration even if:
•	the supplier is an entity of which the recipient is a member, or
•	a supplier only makes supplies to its members.
 
 Example 5: payment by an entity member to an entity
The owners of residential units form a body corporate to manage the affairs of the complex. Each owner contributes $1,100 per year to the body corporate.
The body corporate engages third parties to provide services (for example, lawn mowing, repairs and insurance).
The $1,100 payments made by the unit owners (that is, the body corporate members) are consideration for supplies made by the body corporate to the unit owners.
3.1 Payments and other things that are not consideration
Section 9-17 of the GST Act excludes the following from being consideration or limits the amount of consideration:
•	If a right or option to acquire a thing is granted, then the consideration for the supply of the thing on exercising the right or option is limited to any additional consideration provided. If there is no additional consideration, there is no consideration.
•	Making a gift to a non-profit body is not providing consideration.
•	Certain payments by a government related entity to another government related entity is not providing consideration.
3.2 Supply made for consideration
Paragraph 9-5(a) of the GST Act provides that a supply must be made for consideration for it to be taxable. In other words, there must be a connection or nexus between the supply and the consideration.
It is not necessary for consideration to flow directly between the supplier and the recipient but there must be a close connection (nexus) between the consideration and a supply.
The nexus test
Paragraphs 64 to 72 of GSTR 2001/6 explain our view on the nexus test.
We consider that the expression 'you make the supply for consideration' in paragraph 9-5(a) of the GST Act has a similar meaning to 'there is consideration for the supply that you make'.
In determining whether a payment is consideration under subsection 9-15(1), the test is whether there is a sufficient nexus between the supply and the payment made. The test is an objective test.
Paragraphs 104 to 111 and 180 to 180B of GSTR 2006/9 also explain our view on the required nexus between supply and consideration.
Paragraph 180 refers to our view expressed in other GST rulings which examine the requirement for a nexus between the supply and the consideration.
Those rulings take the view that:
•	the test is objective
•	regard needs to be had to the true character of the transaction
•	an arrangement between parties will be characterised not merely by the description that the parties give to the arrangement, but by looking at all of the transactions entered into and the circumstances in which the transactions are made.
 Attention
Subsection 9-15(2) of the GST Act provides that it does not matter if the consideration is voluntary or not.
 Example 6: voluntary payments (tips) for restaurant supplies
A restaurant run by a sole trader accepts tips from its customers, including tips on bills paid by credit card. These tips are unsolicited and are in addition to the price. The sole trader does not pass these tips on to the restaurant's employees.
The tips are voluntary payments made in connection with the restaurant supplies made by the sole trader. The tips form part of the consideration for those supplies.
On the other hand, if the sole trader passes the tips on to the restaurant's employees, the payments are not for the restaurant supplies by the sole trader. The tips are income of the restaurant employees.
 
