7. Paying GST
7.1 Who is liable for the GST payable?
Section 9-40 of the GST Act provides that you must pay GST on any taxable supply you make.
In other words, the supplier is liable for any GST that is payable.
 Attention
There are special rules that make another entity liable for the GST payable.
We briefly outline some of these in the ‘special rules’ chapter in this product.
7.2 Calculating the GST payable
Section 9-70 of the GST Act provides GST payable on a taxable supply is 10% of the value of that supply.
Commonly, for fully taxable supplies, we calculate GST as 1/11 of the price. For example, if the price of a supply is $22,000, the GST payable is $22,000 × 1/11 = $2,000.
The value of the supply depends on:
•	the price of the supply
•	whether the supply is fully taxable or not.
 Attention
There are special rules about calculating the GST payable.
We briefly outline some of these in the ‘special rules’ chapter in this product.
7.3 The price of a supply
Subsection 9-75(1) defines price as the sum of:
•	any money provided as consideration
•	the GST inclusive market value of anything else provided as consideration.
 
Money
Paragraphs 31 to 46 of GSTR 2001/6 explain our view on consideration expressed as an amount of money.
We consider a promise to pay an amount of money is consideration expressed as an amount of money. For example, if 2 parties set off (in full or in part) mutual debts that were created as a result of mutual supplies for monetary consideration, the consideration for each supply is expressed as an amount of money.
Other consideration
Paragraphs 138 to 158 of GSTR 2001/6 explain our view on how to value non-monetary consideration.
Where the consideration for a supply is non-monetary, the GST inclusive market value of that consideration is used to work out the price and value of the supply. In most circumstances (where parties are dealing at arm's length) our view is the goods, services or other things exchanged are of equal GST inclusive market value.
Market value is regarded as the price that would be negotiated at a specified time between:
•	a knowledgeable and willing but not anxious buyer, and
•	a knowledgeable and willing but not anxious seller acting at arm's length in an appropriate market.
You may determine the GST inclusive market value by applying a reasonable method to arrive at that amount.
Examples of reasonable methods include:
•	the market value of an identical good, service or thing
•	the market value of a similar good, service or thing
•	the market value of the supply
•	a professional appraisal.
 Example 12: market value of a similar good, service or thing
An accountancy firm agrees to prepare the books for a computer programmer for the current financial year. In return, the computer programmer designs a specific computer program for the accountancy firm.
As there is no identical computer program available for valuation purposes, you can refer to the price of a similar computer program performing similar functions.
7.4 Value of a fully taxable supply
Subsection 9-75(1) of the GST Act provides that the value of a fully taxable supply is:
Price × 10/11
 Example 13: value of a fully taxable supply
CarCo Pty Ltd makes a fully taxable supply selling a car to Harry for $22,000.
The value of the supply is 10/11 × $22,000 (the price) = $20,000.
7.5 Value of a partly taxable supply
Partly taxable supplies can be split into 2 categories:
•	supplies that are partly GST-free or input taxed
•	supplies that are partly non-taxable.
Goods and Services Tax Ruling GSTR 2001/8 Goods and services tax: Apportioning the consideration for a supply that includes taxable and non-taxable parts explains our view on apportioning consideration for a supply that includes taxable and non-taxable parts
Supplies that are partly GST-free or input taxed
Section 9-80 of the GST Act deals with supplies that are partly taxable and partly GST-free or input taxed.
Subsection 9-80(1) provides that the value of the taxable supply is the proportion of the actual supply that is taxable.
Subsection 9-80(2) contains the formula for working out the value of the actual supply.
Price of the actual supply × 10 ÷ (10 + Taxable proportion)
‘Taxable proportion’ is the proportion of the value of the actual supply that represents the value of the taxable supply (expressed as a number between 0 and 1)’.
 
The Luxottica case and GSTR 2001/8
The operation of subsection 9-80(2) of the GST Act was tested in Commissioner of Taxation v Luxottica Retail Australia Pty Ltd [2011] FCAFC 20 (Luxottica) in the Full Federal Court.
In effect, the decision in the case made the formula in subsection 9-80(2) inoperable.
Paragraph 81E of GSTR 2001/8 explains the Full Federal Court found the proportion in section 9-80 could not be determined in accordance with the formula in subsection 9-80(2) because the formula involved 2 unknowns:
•	the value of the actual supply
•	the proportion.
Paragraph 81M of GSTR 2001/8 explains that to work out the taxable proportion, the value of the taxable part of the supply is determined by:
•	having regard to the facts and circumstances, and
•	taking a practical, common-sense approach.
The question to be answered is what is a fair and reasonable measure of the value of the taxable part?
Paragraph 81Q explains that, depending on the facts and circumstances in any particular case, a direct or indirect method may be appropriate to determine the value of the taxable part for the purposes of calculating the taxable proportion.
Direct methods
Paragraphs 97 to 108A of GSTR 2001/8 explain our view on some reasonable direct methods of apportionment.
Direct methods use relevant variables that measure the connection between what is supplied (the taxable and non-taxable parts) and the consideration for the actual supply. A direct method usually gives you the most accurate measure of the consideration for (and therefore, the calculation of the value of) the taxable part of the supply you make (that is, the value of the taxable supply).
Such methods may include:
•	the price allocation as agreed between the parties to the supply
•	the comparative price of each part if it were supplied on its own, relative to the whole payment received
•	the relative amounts of rental consideration
•	the relative amount of time required to perform the supply
•	the relative floor area in a supply of property.
Indirect methods
Paragraphs 109 to 111 of GSTR 2001/8 explain our view on some reasonable indirect methods of apportionment.
You may decide it is appropriate you use an indirect method to apportion the consideration for a supply you make.
For example, you may use a reasonable method that includes the addition of an appropriate mark-up to reflect your profit margin relative to the market you supply. Another example is where an arm's length wholesaler or manufacturer makes a supply to you of things that have been packaged in the form in which you will market them. In this case, you may adopt the apportionment ratio worked out by your supplier
Supplies that are otherwise partly taxable
Section 9-80 of the GST Act only applies to supplies that are partly taxable because they are also partly GST-free or input taxed. If a supply is partly taxable for another reason, you apportion the value of the taxable supply under section 9-75.
Paragraphs 81ZJ to 96 of GSTR 2001/8 explain our view on apportionment under section 9-75. Essentially, because there is no formula for apportioning, you apportion on a reasonable basis.
Paragraphs 92 to 113 of GSTR 2001/8 explain our view as to what a fair and reasonable measure of value of the taxable part of the supply in different factual situations is. It depends on the circumstances of each case. In some cases, there will be only one reasonable method you may use.
Depending on your circumstances, you may use a direct or indirect method when apportioning the consideration for a supply.
7.6 Mixed supplies v composite supplies
It is important to differentiate between a mixed and a composite supply as the GST treatment differs.
Neither term is defined in the GST Act.
Paragraph 16 of GSTR 2001/8 explains that a mixed supply is a supply that has to be separated or unbundled as it contains taxable and non-taxable parts that need to be separately identified. Paragraph 17 of GSTR 2001/8 explains a composite supply is a supply that contains a dominant part and includes something that is integral, ancillary or incidental to that part.
You treat a composite supply as a single thing. Paragraph 18 explains that a composite supply is either taxable or non-taxable.
On the other hand, a mixed supply is made up of several individual supplies. A mixed supply is partly taxable and partly non-taxable.
 Example 14: mixed supply
Roberta owns a building comprising both residential and commercial premises. They lease the building to Lawrence who operates a small recruitment agency from the commercial premises and lives in the residential part.
The supply of the residential part is input taxed. The supply of the commercial part is taxable. Roberta is making a mixed supply that is partly taxable and partly input taxed.
 Example 15: composite supply
The Restless Traveller Hotel supplies guests with a picnic box that consists of bread rolls, tomatoes, a packet of cheese slices and bananas. All of these items are GST-free and are packed in a disposable cardboard box.
Also included in the picnic box are some paper serviettes and a plastic knife, that are ordinarily taxable, but in this case, they are insignificant in terms of scale and connection with the food. The serviettes and knife merely contribute to the better enjoyment of the dominant parts of the supply (the food). They are integral, ancillary or incidental to the supply.
The consideration for the picnic box does not need to be apportioned. The entire supply is GST-free.
7.7 Rounding rules
Section 9-90 of the GST Act provides the rules for rounding amounts of GST.
The rounding depends on whether:
•	only one taxable supply is recorded on an invoice, or
•	more than one taxable supply is recorded on an invoice.
 
One taxable supply recorded on an invoice
Subsection 9-90(1) of the GST Act provides that, if there is only one taxable supply recorded on an invoice, the amount of GST is rounded to the nearest cent.
 Attention
You round up 0.5 cents.
This is known as the total invoice rule.
Several taxable supplies recorded on an invoice
Method 1
1.	Calculate the GST payable on each supply without rounding the amount payable.
2.	Add all these amounts together.
3.	Round the total amount to the nearest cent (rounding 0.5 cents upwards)
This is known as the total invoice rule.
Method 2
1.	Calculate the GST payable on each supply without rounding
2.	If an amount has more decimal places than the number of decimal places allowed by your accounting system, round the amount to that number of decimal places
3.	Add the step 1 and step 2 (if applicable) amounts together
4.	Round the amount in step 3 to the nearest cent (rounding 0.5 cents upwards)
This is known as the taxable supply rule.
Choosing between the methods
You may choose whichever method you want to use. In other words, there are no rules requiring you to use a particular method.
 
