Subsequent notices
Now that the correct customer(s) have been identified, subsequent notices can be prepared if additional information and/or documents are required. If multiple matched customers are provided in a response, you will need to issue a separate notice for each one that you require further documents and/or information about.
You will do this by using the New Information Request button within the response. Most fields in the Notice Details and Subject of Enquiry sections will pre-populate, including the receiving financial institution, CRN, and customer details.
Depending on the information and/or documents required, you may need to issue a number of subsequent notices to obtain it. For example, you may request statements in your second notice and then issue a third notice to trace transactions. 
This approach ensures that what we are requesting is relevant and specific to our needs and does not require the receiving financial institution to provide detail that is not specific to our actions.
  
3. Submitting an initial notice
It is now time to run through the process of submitting an initial notice to the financial institution. 
There are four stages to submitting an information notice.
•	Create
•	Save
•	Review
•	Approve or Submit (The approval stage is only used by certain BSLs. Depends on BSL policy.)
Create stage
In this stage, a new Information notice is created on the INGOT system and relevant details are added.
Let’s try the process in INGOT.
Customer Reference Number
The CRN is the financial institution’s customer reference number. It is not a reference held by the ATO and as such you will generally not know the CRN for the entity you are seeking to identify in an initial notice.
In limited circumstances, a financial institution may provide the CRN in a response message to an initial notice, rather than in the response fields. In these cases, you can enter a CRN in an initial notice.
Note: a CRN will always be provided for each matched customer included in a response from a financial institution.
 
Creating an Information Notice in INGOT
 Select the New menu from the yellow navigation bar and select Request for Information from the drop-down menu.
Note, depending on your user group, this may be the only option that appears for you.
The Information Request page consists of three sections.
•	Notice Details
•	Subject of Enquiry
•	Details Required from Recipient
 
Notice Details section 
Use the Notice Details section to specify the purpose, legislation, recipient financial institution and ATO system references relevant to the notice.
Mandatory fields are marked with a red star (*).
Use the Restricted Request check box only where viewing of the request must be restricted, for example, where there is a client or case level SPIN indicator present.
Use the Return highest match only check box if you want the financial institution to return the single customer that best matches the details provided in the Subject of Enquiry section.
Enter the case/activity reference into the System ID.
Information in the Further Info field is not included in the notice issued to the financial institution. You may wish to use this field to include additional references for your own use.
 
Subject of Enquiry section
 
View, edit and delete details about the subject of your enquiry.
Target of Enquiry
This field is mandatory. Enter the name of the entity whose affairs you are examining. In most cases this is the entity that you are auditing or recovering debts from. Importantly the target of enquiry is not always the same as the subject of enquiry.
For example, the target of an enquiry may be an individual, but you may wish to view details of their partner’s accounts to establish any additional sources of income. Details of the partner’s accounts may be requested from the financial institution, but the first individual is the actual target of enquiry.
Important: The details entered in the target of enquiry field are NOT included in the search parameters used by the financial institution. Where you want the entity that is the target of enquiry to be the subject of the financial institution’s search, you must include their details in the Subject of enquiry section.
 
Subject of enquiry
You now need to add the subject of enquiry’s details using the Add… links.
Each entry that is saved will appear in this box. Edit and remove entries with the links on the right-hand side of the box. Each notice must be for a single entity. Separate notices will be required for each subject of enquiry.
You can enter multiple entries for all demographic fields except DOB, Gender, ABN and ACN. For example: 
•	multiple individual names can be included when a single individual may be known by different names:
o	a female that may be known by maiden name or married name, OR
o	an individual that uses an alias as well as their legal name, OR
o	an individual name plus a trading name for a sole trader and all names are linked to a single ABN.
•	multiple organisation names can be included when the entity may be known by different names: 
o	an organisation that has changed its legal name over the course of time, OR
o	the name of the organisation and the trading name, AND
o	all names are linked to a single ABN and/or ACN (Companies only).
An initial notice can include either:
•	a single BSB and account number (BSB is optional) and no other demographics (known as a match type E request) OR 
•	the entity's name(s) plus any other demographics you have (known as a match type D request).
Additional information about match types can be found in the INGOT support material which you can access via the Help function in the yellow navigation bar.
Including all available client demographics will increase the likelihood of a correct match being returned by the financial institution.
Note, providing only a name is insufficient information for the financial institution to identify matching customer(s). If you only provide a name, you will receive a No Match response message.
 
Details Required from Recipient section
 
As this is an initial request, only the Add Question button will be active and generally should not be used.
The Add Question link is only to be used in initial notices:
•	by CAS staff for certain CAS work types
•	by Client Engagement Group or Lodge and Pay staff when instructed by the INGOT support material  or INGOT Administrator.
All notices issued via INGOT contain a standard Schedule requesting the following: 
•	Customer details 
•	Account summaries 
•	Account details 
•	Related parties (the name and role, for example, guarantor, signatory, of any persons, other than the account owner, that are linked to an account) 
•	Security details and Loan details 
 
Save stage
The Information Notice has been created. It must now be saved before being submitted or sent for approval. 
The information notice is saved to the INGOT system. Once saved, it will appear in the My Requests view and can be accessed either from there or by using the Search function.
Saving an Information Notice in INGOT
 
To Save, select the Save button at the bottom of the INGOT page.
If all mandatory fields have been completed, the notice will save successfully, and a green message bar will appear indicating the save was successful.
If mandatory fields have not been completed, an error message will appear at the top of the page identifying which fields require your attention. The fields will be highlighted with a red !.
INGOT ID and Status
When you successfully save a request for the first time, it will be issued with a unique INGOT ID and the status will change from ‘New’ to ‘Draft’. It will remain in ‘Draft’ status until it is sent for approval or submitted to the financial institution.
 Attention 
Remember to save before you navigate away from the Information Request page. If you don’t save, you may lose changes.
 
 
Review stage
Once the notice has been completed and saved, you must review the content prior to sending for approval or submitting to the financial institution.
The Review and Submit button will navigate you to the Review Notice page where you can check the content of a read-only version of the notice prior to submitting it for approval or to the financial institution.
 
The schedule that appears in the notice is comprised of two parts. 
The first five items in the schedule are default items that are in every information notice issued via INGOT and are non-editable.
Any other items in the schedule are determined by the entries made by you in the Subject of Enquiry section of the request and can be edited.
Submit stage
If you are in a business line that does not require approval within INGOT, you can submit the notice directly to the financial institution once you are satisfied that it is correct and complete.
Send your notice
When you have confirmed that the notice is correct and complete, use the Submit to FI button to send your notice to the financial institution. The status of your notice will automatically update to ‘Submission Pending’.
At set times, INGOT runs a batching process of all notices with ‘Submission Pending’ status. Once batching is complete, the status automatically updates to ‘Submitted’, indicating that the notice has now been sent to the financial institution.
Submit button
If you are in a business line that does require approval within INGOT, you will see a Submit for approval button instead of the Submit to FI button on the Review Notice page.
Selecting this button will change the status to ‘Approval Pending’. You must then send an email to the appropriate Approver within your business line and ask that they review and approve the request.
Approving officer
The Approving officer will review the request and either:
•	Approve which will result in the request being submitted to the financial institution with the name of the approving officer being recorded on the notice. The status of the request in My Requests will change from ‘Approval pending to Submitted’.
•	Not approve which will result in the request moving to a status of ‘Not Approved’’ and the approving officer will advise you of the changes to be made prior to you resubmitting for approval. 
4. Updating, withdrawing and recalling notices
Updating notices

 
You can update notices with a status of ‘Draft’ or ‘Not Approved’. Open the My Requests page from the yellow navigation bar to see a list of all your information requests with a status of ‘Draft’, ‘Submission Pending’, ‘Submitted’ and ‘Response(s) received’.
Select the View link for the ‘Draft’ or ‘Not approved’ notice that you want to update, and you will be taken to the Information Request screen. You are now able to update editable fields and add new content.
A notice with a status of ‘Draft’ or ‘Not Approved’ can only be edited or cancelled by its owner or a corporate System Administrator.
After you have updated the notice:
1.	Save the notice.
b.	Review the notice.
c.	Submit to the financial institution or for approval.
 
Recalling notices
If you have submitted a notice to the financial institution and identify that an update is required, you can recall it if the status is ‘Submission pending’.
You will only have a small window of opportunity to recall a notice as they are sent to financial institutions at scheduled times throughout the day. Once the notice has been sent, the status will change to ‘Submitted’.
You cannot recall a notice with a status of ‘Submitted’ and a withdrawal may be necessary.
Withdrawing notices
If you need to withdraw a notice that has a status of ‘Submitted’, you must:
•	contact the INGOT Administrator team, who will advise the recipient financial institution that the notice is withdrawn.
•	use the Manually Close Request button and complete the relevant fields.
