Course description
This course will provide you with an overview of Compliance and Engagement (CE) and Interpretive Assistance (IA) practices that relate to Active case management.
Course details
•	Duration: One hour
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: Compliance and Engagement Group
•	Prerequisites: No prerequisites 
Learning outcomes
After the session, you will have clear expectations for how we should be treated and how our records and information should be managed.
This includes how we:
•	interact with the community
•	manage and record those interactions.
 
1. Introduction
Think about the phrase ‘How we do our work is as important as what we do’.
Putting ourselves in the shoes of our clients helps give us the insight we need to guide our actions and best serve the community.
We can all recall a client experience where we’ve been subject to unexpected delays and kept out of the loop. Have you ever followed up with an organisation and they have no record of the call or correspondence?
We have clear expectations for how we should be treated and how our records and information should be managed. Likewise, the Government and the community expect the ATO to perform to a high standard. This includes how we:
•	interact with the community
•	manage and record those interactions
It’s how we go about our work that makes the different in the client experience.
What is Active case management?
Active case management (ACM) is a set of behaviours and actions designed to deliver appropriate case outcomes for both the client and the ATO. It’s not just about getting work completed by the Siebel due date but all-encompassing in the lifecycle of each case.
There are three core principles in ACM. Select each principle to learn more.
Manage the client relationship
You need to understand the client’s situation including other interactions they may have with the ATO (if any), what is the status of their account, do they have a debt? This preliminary understanding will help inform how you should action your case.
Clients should understand how their matter is progressing and not be surprised by any outcomes. Early and continuous engagement with clients (internal and external) keeps them informed, helps you better understand their circumstances and the impact of your work and helps manage their expectations. 
 
Keep things moving
Plan how to approach the work and prepare for each client interaction.
•	Set calendar reminders to keep track of key dates and manage your workload.
•	If the work item is aged, add a note every month to record progress.
•	Raise issues impacting work completion as soon as they arise
Document our work and interactions
Update Siebel regularly to ensure it provides an accurate and complete record of 
•	all actions taken
•	decisions made
•	key updates, and
•	client interactions.
ACM roles
There are three key stakeholders in ACM. Select each stakeholder to learn more.
Team leader
Your team leader is there to manage your performance and that of your team. They monitor individual and team workflow to maintain momentum and can provide assistance where needed to help progress cases.
They may need to follow up with you where timeframes are in danger of not being met or there’s an opportunity for improvement in how you’re applying ACM principles.
Along with technical advisors, your team leader may have responsibility for approving particular actions/decisions in Siebel.
Case officer
As a case officer your role is key in being the contact point for the client.
You will interact with the client (or their authorised representative) throughout the life of the case.
How you manage this relationship will be critical to the success of your case. Create a collaborative environment where you can. Working collaboratively with our clients allows us to communicate openly and honestly and enables us to develop and maintain the relationship for a purposeful and satisfactory outcome.
Client
Interaction with the client (the taxpayer or their authorised representative) should be ongoing throughout the life of the case. Your approach to each client interaction makes a difference. Every interaction is an opportunity to positively influence the ongoing relationship with the client through your:
•	professionalism
•	client relationship management skills
•	commitment to working effectively with clients, and
•	representation of the Tax Office as an organisation with a strong client focus.
ATO Charter
The ATO Charter (Our Charter) outlines the relationship we seek with the community to administer the tax and superannuation systems. Our Charter provides our commitments, what we ask of clients and steps they can take if they are not satisfied.
You must follow the Charter in all of your dealings with taxpayers. This relationship is based on mutual trust and respect. You can nurture the relationship by:
•	Providing clients with service that is professional, respectful, and timely
•	Treating clients fairly and respectfully
•	Engaging with clients based on their circumstances and previous compliance behaviour
•	Making it as easy as possible for clients to get things right
•	Supporting those who want to properly participate in these systems
•	Being open, transparent, and accountable in our dealings with clients and the community
•	Delivering our services in the most effective and efficient way.
Be mindful of the taxpayers' rights and obligations under the Charter as you action your work.
Getting started in Siebel
You’ll use Siebel to record every aspect of your case.
•	Actions
•	Interactions
•	Issues
•	Decisions
•	Outcomes
•	Communications
•	Creating work items
•	Completing work items
Whether you’re using Work Management or Case Management in Siebel, procedures are available online that provide you step-by-step instructions on how to action and record work correctly.
In Case Management, the IPEC (Initiate, Plan, Execute and Close) model is used to manage our actions and document a case. It is the methodology the Compliance and Engagement Group have in place to ensure a consistent approach to the treatment of clients and aligns with Our Charter and Compliance Model.
Note: Regardless of the Siebel framework used, the three core principles in ACM apply.
 More information
•	Recording the commencement date in Siebel
•	IA – Siebel dates and reporting	
You have a new work item
Consider the following points with every work item.
Get to know the work
Take the time to review any attachments and build your understanding on:
•	what the work is about
•	the reason behind the case selection or client request
•	expected outcomes
•	stakeholder impacts
•	relevant timeframes and benchmarks
•	what is the risk (for CE work)?
Keep track of time
Note: the cycle time for your work item.
It is good practice to set yourself calendar reminders to help you manage your workload and ensure each case is completed on time. Set one a month before the due date for each case to ensure it does not become aged. You can also set them for when your next action is due to take place. You can also use the ‘Next Review’ date field in Siebel.
Consider early exit
Note: The below point only applies for Compliance and Engagement (Compliance) staff.
After conducting a profile of the client and analysing the risks and issues in the case there will be instances where you’ll recommend the case is closed as an early exit.
Each case should be treated on its own merits. You must obtain approval to 'early exit' the case.
An 'early exit' decision is appropriate where you have not tested any of the risks or issues identified for the case and it is determined that it is no longer appropriate to proceed with the case.
Early exit examples
There are at least ten examples where early exit may be appropriate, including:
1.	The case was incorrectly selected or is a duplicate
2.	The case was created using the incorrect case product
3.	Lodgment obligations have been met
4.	The project has ceased
5.	The taxpayer is insolvent or deceased
6.	ATO view or legislation has changed, which impacts the risk
7.	The risk to revenue is considered low
8.	The case is too resource intensive to pursue
9.	The case needs to be escalated to another product
10.	Potential reputation risk to the ATO or community expectations.
 More information
•	Early exit decision approved practice instruction
The early exit CEG approved practice provides a list of scenarios for which early exit may be appropriate.
•	Guiding principles for finalising casework
The guiding principles for deciding whether to finalise a case early for business reasons.

 
2. Plan how you’ll progress
Remember to plan
Planning helps you decide the best course of action, it can help you to:
•	Understand the client and request
•	Identify your stakeholders
•	Identify risks to completing your work in a timely manner
You know the saying 'If you fail to plan, you plan to fail'. Becoming familiar with the details of each case and client helps build a plan for how you will approach the piece of work. Your stakeholders and potential impacts can vary from one work item to another.
Stakeholders
Think about people who need to be involved in completion of your work or be kept informed of your progress.
•	Identify subject matter experts, individuals, groups (panels) and leaders who can help you action your case
•	Understand and manage their expectations
•	Consider how and when you’ll contact them - aim to get the right information to the right people as soon as you can
•	Where other staff need access to your Siebel case you can add them as a participant – for example, your team leader and technical advisor.
Impacts
Think about situations or events that will impact your ability to complete your work.
Situations may include:
•	Staff leave or transfer to another team / work area
•	An uncooperative client
•	Inadequate client records
•	No response to correspondence, or missed deadlines for response
•	Difficulty in obtaining information from third parties.

 
Plan how you’ll action the work
Everyone needs to plan how they’ll approach their case and action the work. To plan effectively and achieve outcomes, you need to be aware of the:
•	Purpose of the work
•	Expected outcomes
•	Tasks involved
•	Resources required
•	Key stakeholders
You may need to document your plan, and have it approved by your team leader or technical advisor before you can contact the client.
Tips and tricks for planning
1.	Manage expectations
You may need to document your plan, and have it approved by your team leader or technical advisor before you can contact the client.
2.	Document and seek approval
Make sure you attach the plan on Siebel.
3.	Case specific format
The format of a case plan can be specific to business lines, products, and market segments. Check you’re using the right one.
4.	Revise your plan
A plan is a living document. As your work progresses you may need to revise your plan.
5.	Consider impacts
Identify and consider potential impacts including the Period of Review (POR) that may affect your progress and how you will manage them.
6.	Capture projected outcomes
You may also need to capture projected outcomes at this point of your work. Refer to your relevant business line procedures for instructions on this. 
 
Communicating with the client
Here’s the key principles to help you effectively manage the client relationship:
•	Engage with the client as early as possible
•	Bring relevant items into initial conversations so they know what to expect
•	Provide regular contact based on your decisions and agreed frequency
•	Be clear - Discuss decisions openly to ensure understanding of the reasoning behind your decision (even if they don’t agree with the decision). Take the opportunity to educate clients to help them get it right in the future
•	Ensure you complete Proof of record ownership (PORO) each time you contact the client, or they contact you. Record it correctly in your contact note/activity in the case.
•	Remember, before you can communicate classified information with a client via email you must inform them of the security risks and obtain their permission to use email
Client Interaction
Effective planning also comes in handy when it’s time to contact the client. Before you contact the client, consider the points you need to cover in each interaction. There are a few examples listed below. Can you think of any others?
Discuss and agree on frequency of updates
This may look different depending on the client and work type.
Understand the client
Make the effort to understand the client and any issues that may impact your work. In CE work, this includes understanding client behaviour and its connection to treatment of penalties. Ensure the client:
•	understands the purpose of each interaction, and
•	is involved in negotiating key aspects that will influence your progress.
Discuss penalties and debt
If you know penalties may apply, discuss this upfront. Similarly, if a debt is likely or there is an existing debt, consider Lodgment Payment and Debt Conversations.
Voluntary Disclosures
Consider if Voluntary disclosures are appropriate and how you’ll treat them.

Reduce the possibility of a dispute
Do what you can to reduce the possibility of a dispute. Where you encounter a dispute consider all options, including Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR). Check if your business line procedures require you to add a dispute indicator.
 Attention 
To use email, follow the 1 to 1 External email corporate guidelines. Before you can communicate classified information with a client via email, you must inform them of the related security risks and obtain their permission to use email. This should be captured in a Siebel case note.
Note: The ATO does not recommend the use of email for OFFICIAL: Sensitive information.
 
3. If it’s not in Siebel, it didn’t happen
Accurate and complete record keeping is vital to:
•	Satisfy the regulatory evidentiary requirements
•	Provide a record of the work conducted, including the evidence and documentation that has been collected and any decisions that have been made and authorised
•	Support effective decision making by having all available information to make informed decisions.
Document your interactions
Managing records
Make sure you have an accurate and complete record of your work. This includes:
•	All records of client communication
•	Records which are material to your work
•	Documenting your observations and findings regarding client behaviour as part of your preliminary research. Remember, it is the taxpayer’s behaviour leading up to and at the time of making a statement (for example, lodging a tax return) that is considered when determining the base penalty amount/s (BPA).
Write for the world to see – Siebel records are subject to freedom of information (FOI) and can be released to the client if requested. Make sure you carefully consider your tone, language, and content.
Where required, create a file to manage your work and maintain it through to completion.
Exceptions
There are certain instances where you cannot or should not attach records to Siebel, including when:
•	It is impractical
The files are physically too large or in a format in which they are not able to be scanned
•	It is inappropriate
Commercial in-confidence documents, legal professional privilege (LPP) documents, suspicious matter reports from AUSTRAC.
•	It is unnecessary
Low or short-term value research or reference records.
 More information
•	CEG document management in Siebel
•	Information asset management CEI
Naming convention
Its important documents are saved using a consistent approach. A single naming convention has been released to apply across the whole of CEG. One naming convention allows:
•	Consistent work practice in the document names attached to Siebel
•	Improved ability to identify documents in a timely manner
•	Auto-sorting of documents in chronological order based on dates an action occurred
•	Transparency regarding the timing of actions.
All material documents should be attached to Siebel using the naming convention:
(Reverse date) _(description)_ (sub-description if required) _ (name of party, where appropriate) _(topic)_ (version number include for updated versions only)
Note: Reverse date means YYYYMMDD. Example: 20231109.
The CEG naming convention guidelines list example document titles you can use for common document types.
 More information
•	CEG naming convention guidelines.
 
Using Siebel notes
Effective note taking is important as it provides transparency on our actions and decision making. Siebel notes provide a sequence of events and a permanent written record to meet accountability requirements.
Notes: must be added every time you interact with a client, take action, or make any decision on the case. This includes recording details of:
•	Conversations, meetings, and interviews
•	Correspondence issued (include date issued and channel used)
•	Requested and received documents
•	Decisions made (and appropriate approvals)
•	Checkpoint and aged case notes.
All notes should be set to Done once they are complete. This ensures they are a permanent record that cannot be changed.
Note: Try to avoid using dot points for recording conversations and meetings as it will be hard to recall the details later.
Usage and access
Your Siebel notes may be accessed for:
•	Client requests (including freedom of information)
•	Complaints and disputes
•	Litigation and prosecution matters
•	Quality assurance.
 More information
•	Siebel Notes approved practice
Correspondence & Responses
Select below to learn more about generating correspondence and receiving responses with clients:
Generating correspondence
•	Use eLetters templates to simplify the creation and tailoring of correspondence to suit the client’s circumstances.
•	If you require a response, be clear on the timeframe for it to be provided by.
•	Each time a letter is created, an activity should be generated in Siebel via the correspondence tab to ensure the correspondence record can be seen on the Siebel client profile (not only on your case).
•	Attach a PDF copy of the issued letter to the correspondence activity. Where required, obtain approval before the letter is issued.
 More information
•	Generating correspondence using eLetters
•	Clarify issues and request further information
•	Our approach to information gathering
•	Your business line procedures for specific rules on generating correspondence.
Receiving responses
Where you have issued a request for information, monitor systems for a response and follow up with the client if it is not provided within the agreed timeframe.
Clients can provide written information via:
•	Email, in accordance with 1 to 1 External email corporate guidelines
•	Mail - rarely used
 More information
•	IA Practice Note - Managing requests for information
•	Obtain relevant information: request by client for extension of time to provide information 

Decisions and outcomes
Ensure decisions are well thought out and supported by evidence. Discuss decisions openly with the client to ensure they fully understand your reasoning (even if they don’t agree).
For Compliance and Engagement work, ensure:
•	The client can respond to your interim decision, and
•	Their views are taken into account when making your final decision
•	Once the decisions and outcomes have been approved you are required to communicate outcomes of the case to the client.
Where you need to issue assessments / amendments or impose penalties:
•	Prepare them in accordance with the relevant instructions. This includes entering the CATs Siebel activity ID to link your Siebel case to ICP. Refer to CATs Enterprise Procedure for more information.
•	Confirm all transactions have been correctly processed and posted to the client’s accounts and all notices have issued to the client. All decisions and outcomes must be captured in the correct templates in Siebel.
Note: The client experience doesn’t always end with the completion of your case. Make sure Siebel is up to date, all information is attached, and notes are completed. This will ensure you are prepared for the possibility of escalation, independent review, or hand over.
 More information
•	Connecting Actions with Transactions (CATs)
•	CATs Enterprise Procedure | Enterprise Knowledge Management

 
4. Check you’ve got it right
Check your progress
It’s important that you continually review your cases and overall workload to make sure you’re staying on track and keeping things moving. If you’re experiencing any difficulty on any case or managing your workload volume, talk with your manager to work out a plan to resolve these issues and get things moving again.
•	Review your planning/milestones regularly. Set calendar reminders to keep on track
•	Conduct and record regular case discussions with your team leader
•	Seek advice from subject matter experts or escalate to other areas of the ATO in a timely manner
•	Where you uncover issues that impact your progress, ensure the client is aware, and contingencies are considered
•	Where a case cannot be completed by the due date consider if it’s appropriate to extend the due date. Any extension to the due date must be discussed with the client, recorded on Siebel, and approved by your manager.
 More information
•	Revised due date & negotiated due date approved practice.
•	IA Practice Note 0713/04 – Siebel dates and reporting
 Check you’ve got it right
We’re all human and we all make mistakes. The Case Validation Tool  (CVT) can help you to make sure everything is recorded correctly in Siebel. It runs multiple tests looking at everything from key dates to the figures in outcomes data. The CVT lets you know if there’s anything that might need to be fixed before you close your case. It does not stop you from closing your case in Siebel. It simply identifies issues you might need to review and resolve before you complete your work.
The CVT focuses on key areas such as timeliness, record keeping and case outcomes for the client and the ATO. It highlights (using indicators) areas of possible high client impact for consideration, focussing on:
•	Client experience
•	Quality of the case and data entered
•	Achieving the right outcome.
The tool works in the same way as a spell and grammar check in a word document. It supports you to identify areas to check and correct, running on both open and closed cases, enabling immediate changes to be made.
It is recommended you run the CVT over your case while in progress.
Pre close check
Have you recorded the case correctly in Siebel?
Before you can close your work item in Siebel, make sure you’ve checked off everything that is needed to ensure your work is correct and complete before closure.
•	All actions, interactions and decisions are accurately reflected in Siebel
•	Case Risks and issues are correct and complete
•	Escalations / intelligence referrals sent (if required)
•	All attachments are published
•	Case Decisions and Outcomes are correct and complete
•	Final client correspondence published and issued
•	All notes and activities are set to Done*
•	Obtained required approvals
•	CVT run over work item to check and correct any errors
•	If case management file used, complete and store / archive appropriately
•	Assessments / Amendments referred for processing (if required)
•	Close your work item.
Some case activities may need to stay open until actions are completed by other areas. There may also be other action you need to monitor before the Case status is set to Closed. Where activities are still open, or you need to monitor actions you can set your case status to Finalised. When all activities can be set to Done and all further action is complete, you can set case status to Closed.
Have you managed the client experience effectively?
To ensure a good client experience, make sure you’ve ticked all the boxes that are relevant to your case. Is there any room for improvement to consider in your next case?
•	Kept client informed as case progressed
