Course description
Welcome to the Audit methodology – Introduction to the IPEC model course.
This course is designed to introduce you to the Initiate, Plan, Execute and Close (IPEC) model. Understanding the IPEC model will help you to effectively manage your audit cases, using an active case management approach. This aligns with the ATO’s IPEC audit methodology.
A high-level overview of case management procedures is provided, with explanatory notes and links to supporting documents.
•	Duration: 1 hour 30 minutes
•	Proficiency level: Foundation
•	Audience: All ATO staff
•	Prerequisites: None
Learning outcomes
At the end of this product, you will be able to:
•	Identify the 4 phases of the IPEC model.
•	List the main tasks undertaken in each phase – Initiate, Plan, Execute and Close.
•	State the purpose of applying an active case management approach to an audit.
1. The ATO approach
Australia’s taxation and superannuation system is based on people cooperating with the ATO and voluntarily complying with their obligations. To help achieve this, we have a responsibility to assist people to understand their rights and obligations.
We also have a responsibility to deal with people who do not meet their obligations under the law. The ATO Charter and the ATO Compliance model are tools that help us select compliance strategies to positively influence client behaviour.
We are focused on working towards achieving greater participation between clients and us by improving communication and by making it easier for clients to be compliant with their tax obligations.
 More information
Find more information on the ATO’s Cooperative approach here.
Our clients
Our taxation and superannuation system relies on clients assessing their liability under the law and paying the correct amount of tax (self-assessment).
Self-assessment has inherent risks, and we manage these risks by balancing our resources and structuring ourselves to help people meet their obligations.
We are continually seeking new ways to help our clients participate in the tax system. We believe this should include advice and education on how to fix errors in their statements and lodgments so that they can get it right in the future.
By reshaping our approach to engaging with our clients, we want to actively encourage people to willingly participate in the tax and superannuation system.
ATO Charter
Our Charter outlines the relationship we seek with the community and explains our commitments and what we ask of clients. It provides the steps clients can take if they are not satisfied with our decisions or actions.
 
Client behaviour
Client behaviour is influenced by business, industry, technology and data, sociological, economic and psychological factors. These broad categories of factors influence the extent to which a client chooses to or can meet their obligations.
 
Business
Tax obligations can vary depending on the nature, size and structure of a business. The business factors include:
•	Business structure: what type of entity is it (for example, sole trader, partnership or company)? Complex structures are likely to have more complex affairs.
•	Business activity: where are they located and what is the focus – local, national or international?
•	Length of operation: how long have they been trading? New small business clients may not have as good an understanding of their tax obligations compared to those with many years of experience.
•	Level of sophistication: how advanced and/or complex is the management practices and business operations and strategies? How efficient is the business?
•	Financial position: can they hire tax accountants and lawyers to make sure they get things right? Can they pay their debts? Businesses facing financial hardship may find it difficult to pay their tax liabilities on time.
 
Industry
The industry factors include:
•	Type of industry: the seasonal nature, number of cash transactions, cost of inputs, ability to claim GST credits can affect the level of compliance.
•	Size and segment.
•	Industry regulation.
•	Competition: if there is strong competition, businesses are more likely to avoid tax to obtain a competitive advantage.
•	Labour issues.
•	Infrastructure.
Sociological
The sociological factors include:
•	Age: when was the person born? How old are they? For example, clients new to the tax system may not yet have a good understanding of their tax obligations.
•	Gender: international research has indicated that tax systems have different impacts on men and women – women tend to be more compliant.
•	Social norms: how and where do they fit into society? What are the norms of the group they belong to?
•	Education: the amount and quality of a person’s education may affect their ability to understand complex tax matters.
•	Ethnic background: is English a second language? Are they from a country with a different tax system? These factors can lead to misunderstandings about their tax obligations.
Economic
The state of the economy and changing economic times influences business profitability and personal finances. The economic factors include:
•	Inflation: people are more likely to reduce expenses when interest rates or inflation is high.
•	Interest rates.
•	Taxation and superannuation system: the complexity of the taxation and superannuation system may make it difficult for clients to understand their obligations.
 
Psychological
The things that make a person ‘tick’ – their attitudes, fears and values play a part in compliance behaviour. The psychological factors include:
•	Risk: to what extent is the person a risk taker? Those adverse to risk are more likely to comply.
•	Fear.
•	Trust: what has been their previous interactions and experience with the taxation and superannuation system? Do they believe it is fair and equitable? People are more likely to comply if they believe the system is fair and well administered.
•	Values.
Technology and data 
How clients engage with and use available technology and data can impact how they meet their legal obligations.
Technology and data factors include: 
•	Understanding the systems that are used to manage record keeping requirements. Do they have electronic systems in place? 
•	What level of maturity do they have in their sales, payment and record keeping systems?
•	Do they have appropriate integrity safeguards in place and maintenance of their systems to ensure alignment with legislative requirements?
•	Ability to participate in Computer assisted verification: e-Audit and risk assessment.
The compliance model
The compliance model helps us to understand the different factors that influence compliance behaviour. This enables us to choose the most appropriate action for the circumstances. The compliance model is based on the belief that our actions can influence clients' compliance behaviour.
 
It shows a continuum of client attitudes towards compliance. At the base of the continuum, clients have the desired attitude of being 'willing to do the right thing'. At the other extreme, clients have decided not to comply – choosing to evade or opt out of the tax system.
Active case management
You need to apply active case management (ACM) practices to ensure cases are progressed in a timely and effective manner with consideration to the client experience and all records are accurate and complete in Siebel. For further information on active case management, see CEG Active case management L&D package and CEG Active case management procedure.
Where cases aren’t progressed appropriately the ATO may have to remit interest due to delays caused by case officer inactivity. Refer to Law Administration Practice Statement 2006/8 Remission of shortfall interest charge and general interest charge for shortfall periods.
The IPEC model
The IPEC model is a 4-phased project management approach to managing audit case work. It is used to manage, action and document an audit case. 
Applying the IPEC methodology ensures a consistent approach to the treatment of clients. It aligns with Our Charter and Compliance model.
The phases of the IPEC model are:
•	Initiate – where a general understanding is gained of what the case is about and why it was selected.
•	Plan – Prepare the case plan.
•	Execute – Conduct and manage the case.
•	Close – Finalise the case.
2. Initiate phase
The purpose of the initiate phase of a case is to gain a general understanding of what the case is about and why it was selected. 
Starting an audit case
The purpose of this step is for you to gain an understanding of what you are being asked to do and the timeframe within which you are expected to do it.
To start an audit case, you need to gain an understanding of the following information:
•	the activities and risks that have been identified 
•	the reason why a particular approach (case product or activity) has been selected
•	the appropriate treatment of the identified risk based on their assessment
•	the timeframes and benchmarks necessary to ensure the case is actively managed.
 Attention 
Refer to your team leader for further assistance.
Case commencement activities 
Once the cycle time of the audit case has been determined, this is recorded on the case ‘Details’ view in Siebel. This tells you how many calendar days have been allowed for the 'end to end' process for the case.
You will need to set the commencement date to record the date on which you started the first activity in the case.
It’s important to set timeframes as you are required to communicate the expected case completion date to the client. This will be the due date displayed in Siebel when the commencement date is the same as the allocation date. To work out the expected case completion date, add the cycle time to the commencement date.
The 'check ATO activity' task is a reminder to co-ordinate your compliance activity with other internal stakeholders, if applicable, so that we do not impose unnecessary requirements on the client.
It is also an opportunity to find out if we have already asked the client for the information you need to progress your case.
 
Siebel case management
Siebel case management is system used to record all case actions and interactions such as phone calls, interviews conducted, discussions with your team leader, dates of issuing letters, notes on referrals to other areas, actions taken applying methods and active case management discussions in the case.
The Case – add, update or view a Siebel case note procedure details how to use the Notes view.
 More information
Refer to Siebel notes approved practice for further guidance.
Voluntary disclosures
Voluntary disclosures give the clients the opportunity to correct their tax affairs. Making a voluntary disclosure can reduce penalties associated with providing incorrect information.
Voluntary disclosures help with tax administration as they can save the ATO time and resources.
A voluntary disclosure arises where a taxpayer:
•	has advised of an error or omission in a statement that leads to an increased tax related liability (shortfall amount) for an accounting period
•	provides information to the Commissioner in an approved form sufficient for the Commissioner to identify the shortfall amount.
 More information
The voluntary disclosure approved practice provides more information on voluntary disclosures.
3. Plan phase
The second phase of the IPEC model is the Plan phase. You will develop a case plan for all audits conducted. This is the part where you work out what tasks you have to complete to address the compliance risks associated with the case and the timeline for completing those tasks by the expected completion date.
Spend the time required to plan. Discuss your plan with your approving officer while you are developing it.
Your first priority must be to determine why an audit is required and provide reasonable, lawful and clear reasons behind your decision as to why you are (or are not) proceeding.
Refer to your relevant business line procedures that must be followed in the planning of an audit.
Profile the client
Profiling is about gaining an understanding of the client and their business environment. This involves reviewing client information held in ATO systems.
You can review this information using Enterprise Client Profile (ECP) or RAPT System Support Page, which provides access to a range of profiles of client information held on ATO systems and BAS information can be explored using BAS Analyser.
The Information Hub provides access to A-Z Database listing and company and industry research. 
You can also check if the client has an internet page with details of who they are and what they do.
Profiling the client will help you to establish and assess their compliance behaviour, where they are placed in the Compliance model and if it’s appropriate to use Computer assisted verification: e-Audit and risk assessment.
You must ensure that you complete and attach a profiling report to your Siebel case.
 Attention
Check your business line’s instructions for a profiling guide appropriate to your market segment. In some instances, the client profile may be completed using automation technology. 
Risk mitigation
Anticipating a client's position in relation to the risk or how the client may respond to requests for information or other problems which might emerge during an audit will help you respond more effectively.
Some things to consider when determining this:
Compliance risk
•	What are the indicators of the compliance risks?
•	Where does the client sit in the ATO Compliance model?
•	What are the implications for the case plan? 
Risk methods
Given the nature of the business:
•	What type of direct and indirect testing of the risk will be appropriate?
•	What risk methods are appropriate for testing the risks?
•	What records and information will you need to gather to complete testing of the risks? 
•	Do we already have the required information?
Operational risk management
•	Will you need assistance from any subject matter experts?
•	What operational risks need to be managed?
•	How will you manage operational risks?
Personal security risk management
•	What personal security risks need to be managed when working with clients offsite?
•	How will you manage personal security risks? 
•	Complete the appropriate working outside the ATO checklist for field work.
More information
For more information see:
•	Keeping safe with clients.
•	Working outside the ATO field checklist.
•	ATO Managing Inappropriate Client Behaviour Framework.
Tracking your progress
•	What milestones do you need to set to help you track progress and complete the audit case by the expected completion date?
Dispute risk
Dispute risk is the risk that an audit will progress to objection and subsequent litigation.
All CEG staff must incorporate dispute minimisation approaches into their work and consider opportunities to engage with clients early to resolve disputes where possible.
The first step is to effectively identify the dispute risk. In some cases, a dispute risk activity generated from system analytics may be added to your Siebel case.
In all cases you need to be alert to the presence of indicators that suggest the risk of a dispute arising with the client, for example:
•	The client's behaviour during the audit, for example the client seeks legal advice on the matters that are subject to audit.
•	The client or their representative tells you that they do not agree with your findings.
•	The proposed adjustment is a significant amount for the client.
Cases identified with dispute risk should be closely monitored to ensure that:
•	Technical decision making processes are sound and preparatory work is of the highest quality.
•	Where appropriate, specialist advice is sought.
•	Other dispute resolution strategies are considered, including Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR).
Information and file management
The ATO has strict guidelines for managing paper documents or electronic records and these guidelines vary depending on where you work.
Different work areas within the ATO may have additional requirements, guidelines and record keeping practices according to the Information asset management Chief Executive Instruction (CEI).
Case and file management principles are linked to many documents providing instruction and guidance on effective case management including:
•	Our approach to information gathering.
•	ATO Compliance model.
•	CEG Document management in Siebel.
•	Computer assisted verification: e-Audit and risk assessment.
 
Plan the audit case
Case plans demonstrate the reasoning behind case selection and the proposed actions to be taken to complete it. A properly constructed case plan will detail the outcomes that are sought to be achieved and what resources will be needed to assist in completing the audit.
Establish a proposed timeline based on the benchmarks for the type of audit you are planning to conduct.
Consider the guiding principles for finalising case work for judgment decisions that you will make in deciding whether to finalise a case early for business reasons.
Consider the elements of task management:
•	The projected timeline – use time management to set critical milestones by which certain tasks should be completed.
•	The need to clarify issues and request further information – identify how long it might take to obtain that information.
•	Any relevant resource issues – identify the need for collaboration with other technical staff and across business lines.
•	Fraud and risk management – identify the need to escalate issues or disputes.
•	Client relationship management in accordance with Our Charter and the ATO's cooperative approach. Your planning will lay the groundwork for the remainder of the case, ensuring it is appropriately resourced, and that all the necessary tasks to achieve the desired outcomes are identified.
 More information 
Your plan is a ‘living’ document that allows for changes to be made should circumstances dictate. Such changes may require sign off from your approving officer before being implemented.
Recording facts and evidence 
Correctly gathering, establishing and recording the facts of a case are an integral part of good case management. It is also an integral part of supporting quality, legally defensible decisions or positions.
A fact is any act, occurrence or thing which is relevant to an issue that can be proved by primary direct evidence. A fact is established by presenting evidence which shows that the fact exists. Facts are relevant in determining the outcome of an issue.
The Facts and evidence worksheets (FEW) are a useful tool to derive the link between a client's situation and the law. Identifying the correct facts is critical to applying the law and forming an ATO position because they determine whether the provision either applies or does not apply.
The FEW is used for identifying and recording the facts and evidence gathered for each specific issue. The FEW is to be used for substantive tax issues and will be prescribed by business line procedures. In cases where the FEW is not used, the facts and decisions of a case should be recorded in the relevant Siebel tabs.
If further assistance is required on the use and completion of the FEW, consult your team leader, technical leader or risk manager.
 More information 
Refer to Evidence: Facts & evidence worksheets (FEW) training for an overview of the worksheets and their application.
Client relationship management
An audit management plan will be prepared and provided to the client that informs them about the proposed timeline and what their role in the process will be. Consider the information and documents you will need, including a reasonable timeframe for the client to provide them. Refer to your business line procedures for requirements.
Lay the foundation for a cooperative relationship with the client by ensuring your intended course of action is clearly outlined and discussed at the commencement of the case.
Your case planning should include these integral components:
•	Consider Privacy and taxpayer confidentiality factors and their effect
•	Devise a communication strategy – this will depend on the client and their profile in the Compliance model
•	Be aware of and factor in possible time delays.
Take the opportunity to assist our clients to understand their compliance obligations and to influence their compliance choices. This includes their obligations not within the scope of your examination. This helps us to build a relationship in an environment where the client is aware of the compliance choices they have and the consequences of those choices. 
 More information 
For further information refer to Client relationship management. 
Sign-off the case plan
Discuss your case plan with your approving officer and obtain their approval if required, before you contact the client.
Complete the following items before requesting approval:
•	Set case status to 'In Progress'.
•	Set case phase to 'Plan'.
•	List all compliance risks and issues in the ‘Risks & Issues’ view.
•	Attach your case plan (where applicable).
•	Complete the Projected Outcomes (where applicable).
•	Record the:
-	‘Period Begin’ and ‘Period End’ dates.
-	Cycle time/commencement date in case ‘Details’ view in Siebel.
At the completion of the planning stage, you will have an idea of the way in which you will conduct the case and have recommendations for your approving officer.
 More information 
Business lines have specific case plans relevant to the different ATO products and market segments. You should refer to your business line’s procedures when planning your case. 
These are accessible in Enterprise Knowledge Management.
4. Execute phase
The third phase of the IPEC model is the execute phase. Two vitally important aspects of success in this phase are relationship management and active case management.
Documenting all facts, evidence, decisions and outcomes in a timely manner and in accordance with ATO policies and guidelines is essential.
Having real time engagement with the client and being transparent with what you’re seeing and what you’re trying to understand are critical aspects of the execution phase of an audit.
 More information 
The CEG Active case management course will assist with this phase of your audit.
Managing information
Work cooperatively with the client
The ATO’s preference is to undertake reviews and audits on an informal basis as part of a cooperative approach. This means that you ask the client to provide information and records voluntarily, rather than invoking our powers.
ATO's preferred cooperative approach 
A cooperative approach is at the heart of Our Charter and necessarily involves cooperation between you and the client and their representative.
You need to balance the cooperative approach, with the need to meet the milestones and timeframes. In effect, it is about managing the case – keeping control of the progress to ensure that you achieve the desired outcomes.
In deciding how best to go forward you should consult with your team leader, mentor, or consider contacting your business line's information gathering expert.
 Attention
Whatever actions you take to progress the case, you must treat the client in a fair and reasonable manner.
Contemporaneous notes
It is extremely important that you keep contemporaneous notes. During your case you will be talking to the client, their representative and possibly third parties. It is essential that you make a record of all these interactions.
You must ensure that:
•	your notes are timely, accurate and informative
•	your notes are legible, brief, and logical
•	you use plain English and your language is clear and unambiguous.
Forming a hypothesis
Your initial risk hypothesis of the risks associated with the audit will be determined, articulated and recorded at the start of an audit case, but should be subjected to regular reviews and refinements. Any significant changes to a risk hypothesis should be communicated in writing to the client in a timely manner.
A good hypothesis should:
•	be written as a definite statement
•	be based on observations and knowledge
•	be capable of being tested with purposeful enquiry
•	clearly state the area of potential non-compliance
•	be capable of being expressed in writing.
 
Elements of risk
Typical elements to review and incorporate into a risk hypothesis:
•	Financial or tax performance that varies substantially from industry trends or patterns.
•	Significant variations in the amounts or patterns of tax payments compared with past performances.
•	Relevant economic indicators and industry trends.
•	Unexplained variation between economic performance, productivity and tax performance.
•	Unexplained losses, low effective tax rates.
•	Businesses or entities that consistently pay relatively little or no tax.
•	History of aggressive tax planning.
•	Promotion of tax exploitation schemes.
•	Businesses experiencing rapid growth, restructure, mergers or de-mergers, deploying new accounting software or undergoing changes to accounting staff.
•	Businesses employing strategies that may lead to an erosion of the corporate tax base.
Each tax risk has an underlying risk value. This risk value is most often represented by the total value of the risk (measured in tax dollars) and the resulting tax at risk (the tax effect of the total value of the risk).
Risk quantification
When quantifying a tax or super risk, the following should be considered:
•	Risk involving multiple years.
•	Risk that extends over several years.
•	Risk in one year but may also have consequences for other years (for example, carry forward losses).
•	Risk involving more than one event, including where a number of events interact together to create a tax or super risk (risks involving transfer pricing usually comprise a series of events).
•	Tax risk with a notional tax impact.
•	Tax rebates or credits are disallowed.
 
Gathering information and evidence
When you have established that a compliance issue exists, you need to consider what evidence is needed to confirm this assertion. Refer to Our approach to information gathering and specifically to the section covering Limits to our formal powers.
Even with the onus of proof resting with the client, the ATO cannot, and does not, disregard the necessity of obtaining evidence to support its case. In practical terms, the client can discharge their onus of proof by giving evidence under oath, which may be accepted by the court as proof unless there is strong evidence to rebut their assertions.
To support your case, you need evidence that confirms the existence of a compliance issue. You must follow your business line’s procedures for capturing such evidence. If documents have not already been scanned, you need to scan them and attach them to your Siebel case.
You may be required to contact third parties to obtain information or documentation. In most cases you should advise the client that you will be contacting a third party before doing so. However, there may be circumstances where you do not advise the client prior to contacting the third party. You should follow corporate guidance on making Third party enquiries.
To assist with identifying and recording relevant facts and evidence, and if prescribed by business line procedure, the FEW are used. 
 More information
To learn more about what evidence is, take the Evidence – Introduction course.
Legal Professional Privilege
Legal Professional Privilege (LPP) protects confidential communications between a client and a lawyer from being disclosed. LPP does not apply to every communication to or from a lawyer, or to every document produced by a lawyer.
LPP only applies to those confidential communications between a client and the client’s lawyer if the dominant purpose of the communications is, either:
•	To enable the client to seek, or the lawyer to provide, legal advice.
•	For use in existing or anticipated litigation.
LPP is a concept that clients can claim in relation to documents you may wish to obtain from them and is also available to the ATO when it provides legal advice to its employees relating to the execution of their duties.
There are strict procedures to follow when LPP is claimed by a client or when dealing with internal ATO documents.
Some sensitive documents are not to be attached to a Siebel case because they are subject to LPP.
In this context, LPP is the right of the ATO to maintain confidences, and to protect certain communications, discussions, written materials or other information which by their nature are privileged from disclosure to externals, third parties and the courts.
The privilege belongs to the Australian Taxation Office, not our legal advisors or information providers.
 More information
For more information see Legal professional privilege method.
Testing your hypothesis
To ensure that all aspects of the hypotheses have been accurately and thoroughly tested, it may be necessary to carry out testing with internal ATO experts, such as technical advisors, or seek legal opinions (from the Tax Counsel Network) for any case that present a greater risk or be a contentious case.
What to test?
•	Take into account new information received.
•	Take into account new identified risks.
Proceed or Exit?
Reduce cost to the client by not proceeding to audit for every case or issue.
Scope
•	Determine whether there are any compliance issues that require a more in-depth investigation and response.
•	Determine whether to focus on one or more specific risks or review the entire business operations.
 
Interacting with the client
Phone contact
You may need to contact the client or their authorised representative, such as a tax agent about the case. Ensure you check who the appropriate person to contact is before you do so.
In most circumstances, regardless of who you contact, the initial contact is done by phone. During this phone contact, you must:
•	identify yourself
•	use the Outbound proof of record ownership (PORO) guidelines
•	confirm that you are talking to the client or their authorised representative (as appropriate)
•	explain to the client or authorised representative what the area of risk and concern is.
For further guidance see Employee Identification CEI which sets out the way we identify ourselves to clients and contributes to the professional service we commit to under Our Charter. 
Face-to-face interviews
Before beginning any face-to-face interview, you need to show your ATO identification to the person you are interviewing and then confirm their identity as being either the client or their authorised representative.
Should further information about your identity be requested to assure the person that they are dealing with a bona fide employee of the Australian Taxation Office - Employee Identification CEI provides details about what to do in this situation.
Best practice when conducting face-to-face interviews is to have an interview plan prepared, with a number of questions that you would like further information about relating to the audit.
You can get further help in preparing for the interview by undertaking the Audit interviews – the basics course.
 
Compliance risks
Analyse all available information (including any voluntary disclosures made) to identify potential compliance risks and/or issues.
Research electronically to identify any existing ATO view for the risks identified, including searching risks and issues in Siebel. Identify and confirm the potential compliance risks, regulatory requirements and/or issues. 
Consider the risk hypothesis:
•	determine materiality of risks and update the case
•	quantify the revenue at risk
•	document findings
•	refine the initial risk hypothesis if necessary and discuss any refinements before communicating in writing any significant changes to the client
•	note discussion and attach any correspondence to case file (paper or electronic).
You may generate and evaluate multiple hypotheses during the planning stages of an audit because of the preliminary and ongoing risk assessments.
Any significant changes to a risk hypothesis should be communicated in writing to the client in a timely manner.
Sampling and testing
Rather than checking every transaction or process a client uses, we sample a selected number, based on examining perceived risk areas. Sampling allows you to test and measure the effectiveness of a client’s internal controls to gather audit evidence to meet your case objectives.
A control testing worksheet has been developed to assist you to document your approach, actions and conclusions relating to the sampling process. The worksheet and its contents will form part of the working papers and evidence from which the audit conclusions are made.
 
The aim of this methodology is to use sampling as a means of objectively:
•	Testing the effectiveness and strength of the client’s internal controls.
•	Determining the level of confidence that can be placed on the client’s accounting system and business processes.
•	Identifying potential errors and risk factors contributing to errors such as manual intervention, decision-making points, control breakdowns etc.
•	Identifying documents, reports and data available as sources of audit evidence and potential sampling populations.
•	Conducting an efficient and effective audit, by more accurately targeting your testing to areas, of control weakness by detecting potential errors resulting from the client's current internal controls.
Making a decision
The ATO’s good decision-making model sets out the principles which need to be satisfied when making sound decisions. Your decisions must be fact and evidence based. They must be reasonable and comply with the principles of natural justice.
At the completion of this stage of your case you should have gathered all the necessary information and evidence to have reached a decision about the client’s compliance and be prepared to make recommendations.
It is important that you discuss your recommendations, including penalty considerations, with your team leader or mentor. They will either confirm your decision or identify possible deficiencies that might be present in your case.
You may need to request further information from the client to clarify issues or establish a more robust case.
 More information
Find out more about the ATO technical decision making model.
 
Determining compliance behaviour
Determining compliance behaviour is a critical aspect of compliance work as it affects any penalty that may be imposed if a shortfall is determined.
Ask the client questions that will assist you to determine whether or not they are complying, such as:
•	Who prepares certain documents or maintains business records?
•	How and why they treated transactions in the way they did?
Penalties
The imposition, remission and cancellation of administrative penalties
If you consider that penalties, interest charges or remissions are applicable, consult the relevant methods for guidance on the imposition, remission and cancellation of administrative penalties. These are available on the Penalty and Interest bundle and include:
•	Penalties method.
•	General interest charge.
•	Shortfall interest charge.
There are several rulings, practice statements and guides that can assist you to determine the level of penalty to impose and include:
•	Miscellaneous Taxation Ruling MT 2008/1: Penalty relating to statements: meaning of reasonable care, recklessness and intentional disregard.
•	Practice Statement Law Administration PS LA 2012/4: Administration of false or misleading statement penalty – where there is no shortfall amount.
•	Practice Statement Law Administration PS LA 2012/5: Administration of false or misleading statement penalty – where there is a shortfall amount.
 More information
For penalty decisions, you may need to complete and attach (to Siebel) the relevant Facts and evidence worksheets. See your BSL procedures for further information.
 
Payment conversations
You are expected to initiate a payment conversation with the taxpayer where a liability is raised at the end of an audit. Engaging the client early helps set the expectation of collection.
As part of the ATO's preferred cooperative approach, it is of paramount importance that you continue to consider the taxpayer's rights and obligations and the way you conduct yourself, refer to our principles for our approach to information gathering and Our Charter. 
 More information
The CEG Lodgment, Payment and Debt conversation guidelines provide guidance and support in addressing payment of liabilities with the client.
Informing the client
Once your approving officer has approved your initial findings and recommendations, you should discuss these with the client.
Factors such as the complexity of the issues you have identified, as well as the procedures of your business line will influence the way in which you communicate your findings to the client.
Simple matters may only require a phone call. More complex matters may mean arranging an exit interview.
Your discussion with the client should include the following:
•	What action you are taking – for example, no further action, revision or amendment.
•	The amount of any proposed revisions to the activity statements, or any amendments to tax returns.
•	The reason for any proposed revisions or amendments – if you have prepared a position paper it should form the basis of the discussion.
•	If the revision is limited to the initial examined period only, advise the client that no further investigation is necessary.
•	Where self-adjustment is allowed and offered – advise the client about this.
•	Issues surrounding the imposition or remission of any penalty.
•	The imposition or remission of any interest charges.
•	A payment conversation at the end of the audit if you are raising a liability.
This is also an opportunity for the client to provide any submissions they may have in relation to the decision you have made.
 
Written notification
In some instances, you may issue a position paper to provide the client with the opportunity to respond to your initial findings. This is normally done by providing the client with a letter which includes a position paper either in the body of the letter or as an attachment.
You may want to refer to these resources before communicating with your client.
•	Outbound capability eLetters.
•	Position paper method.
•	ATO Outbound Interactions – Style and Implementation Guide.
•	ATO style guide.
•	Outbound Communications in the ATO CEI.
Client response
Provide the client with reasonable opportunity to consider and respond to your initial findings and recommended decisions. Agree to a date by which the client will respond. A submission may be made either verbally or in writing and should be provided within an agreed timeframe. The timeframe will depend on the complexity of the issues but should not be excessive.
When you have the client's response, consider any new information and determine what impact it has on your recommendations.
Manage disputes
Be prepared to manage disputes with the client over facts or findings.
This may involve settlement negotiations or Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) processes.
Where contentious issues cannot be resolved, the client may request an Independent review.
 
Recording your findings
Recording your findings should be done on a continual basis throughout the case. This may include recording required information in relevant reports and/or in Siebel. See your business line procedures for further information.
The final case report will include the following points (as applicable):
•	reason for the audit
•	background of the audit and history of client
•	description of financial affairs
•	books of account and financial institution details
•	scope of audit
•	items examined
•	evidence
•	client’s explanation
•	items in dispute
•	items adjusted
•	penalty submission
•	liaison with other areas
•	capacity to pay
•	prosecution considerations
•	recommendations.
All documents will need to be recorded and attached to the case. Additionally, during this phase referrals are made to necessary areas based on the findings of the case.
 More information
For case procedures specific to your work area, refer to Enterprise Knowledge Management.
Completing administrative steps
Case decisions
Case decisions must adhere to the principles set out in the ATO technical decision making model. The ATO’s preference is to undertake examinations on an informal basis as part of a Cooperative approach in Our approach to information gathering 
If you have followed active case management principles and kept the taxpayer informed of progress during the case, your findings should not come as a surprise to the client.
Obtaining final approvals
Discuss your final recommendations and case outcomes with your team leader or approving officer. Obtain approval from the appropriate authorised officer before you close the case.
The approving officer is the legal decision maker and their decision must be able to withstand internal and external scrutiny.
When they approve a case officer's recommendation, they are declaring that the decision is correct (substantive issue and penalty and interest) and all procedures have been properly followed, so it is of utmost importance that you have followed procedure and made reasonable, justifiable and defensible recommendations.
Communicate outcomes to client
You must communicate the outcomes and issue approved finalisation letter to the client soon after approval of the decisions. 
Conduct your exit interview (either over the phone or face to face) to present your final position to the taxpayer. The exit interview is also an opportunity to discuss with the taxpayer the issue of payment of any additional tax liabilities, if applicable.
Adjustments
You may be required to make adjustments in accordance with your decisions and this may impact on penalties. The imposition and remission of penalties and charges are not considered until after the taxpayer has had an opportunity to consider and respond to our audit position.
Further information can be found in the Penalty and Interest bundle page.
For further learning see the ATO learning platform and search for ‘Penalties’.
Escalations
You may also be required to escalate any intelligence gathered for further investigation. For case procedures specific to your work area, refer to Enterprise Knowledge Management.

5. Closing the case

The fourth phase of the IPEC model is the close phase. It is where the objectives, actions and outcomes are concluded.
It is important to ensure that:
•	that final actions occur
•	any outstanding items are resolved
•	all administrative steps have been completed
•	all objectives are achieved
•	any final authorisations and delegations have been obtained
•	all processing has correctly been completed including account postings, issuing of notices for assessments, amendments and penalties and charges
•	all referrals and escalation to other areas for action have been done
•	ATO record-keeping and file management procedures are followed.
The Case Validation Tool (CVT) supports staff to identify areas in the Siebel case to check and correct. This tool is generally used prior to closing your case to identify any potential errors, issues or omissions to be fixed. 
 More information
For further learning see the Case validation tool course.
Record-keeping and file management
It is of paramount importance that you manage records and information gathered through your casework in an appropriate and prescribed manner.
The Information asset management CEI applies to all case documents.
For guidance on complying with your record management responsibilities, refer to:
•	CEG Naming convention guidelines.
•	CEG Document management in Siebel.
•	Protecting information.
•	Records management.
•	Security CEI.
 
Closing the case
Closing is the final process for case completion. Only the case owner can update the case status to ‘Closed’ in Siebel. Once the case status is set to ‘Closed’ the case becomes read only.
When your team leader has approved your request to close the case, follow Request case closure procedure to close the case.
Before closing your case, ensure all these actions have been completed:
•	Case risks, issues, decisions and outcomes have been addressed in accordance with business procedures.
•	‘Open’ activities in the Activities list view have an appropriate status.
•	All notes in the ‘Notes’ view have the status set to ‘Done’.
•	Documents attached to the case have been approved (where necessary) and published.
•	All processing has been correctly completed including account postings, issuing of notices for assessments, amendments, penalties and charges.
•	Referrals or escalation to other areas have been made.
Post audit 
An objection may be lodged by the client to review a decision made in the audit.
It is expected that the evidence and reasons supporting the original decision will have already been provided and explained to the client. This should form part of the case records of the decision and be accessible to objection officer to assist in resolving the objection.
 More information
The Dispute or object to an ATO decision provides further information. 
There is also additional information on taxpayer rights provided in Our Charter about their right to object. 
These are all designed to assist you with this process.
Training recap
Conclusion
Congratulations, you have completed this course. The course provided a high-level overview of case management procedures, with explanatory notes and links to supporting documents that align with the ATO’s IPEC methodology.
The activities required to complete an audit case align with the IPEC phases associated with active case management in the ATO.
The Australian taxation and superannuation system is based on everyone cooperating with the ATO and voluntarily complying with their legal obligations. To help achieve this, we are responsible for assisting people to understand their rights and obligations. We must also deal with people who do not meet their legal obligations. 
It is important to note that this is an introductory course. The information you have learned helps in beginning your journey to becoming a proficient Compliance and Engagement Officer.
There are many other training courses to complete, as well as becoming familiar with legislation, ATO policy, and local work practices.
Start consolidating your learning by undertaking appropriate case work and discussing your learnings with team leaders, subject matter experts and colleagues. After you have begun practising these concepts, you will be ready to undertake more formal training.
What you have learnt
You should now be able to:
•	identify the 4 phases of the IPEC model
•	list the main tasks undertaken in each phase – Initiate, Plan, Execute and Close
•	state the purpose of applying an active case management approach to an audit.

Completion of learner material
