Job Aid - Profiling on the job

Overview on the construction and analysis of profiles to identify potential risks and issues

Last published 26 June 2026

Favourite Notes 
Background and purpose

Multi-Risk Assurance (MRA) is responsible for identifying and treating behaviours that are likely to lead to small businesses failing to meet their obligations. We focus on those taxpayers who have the greatest opportunity to evade their obligations by omitting to report some or all of their income, claiming incorrect deductions and whose lifestyle, spending and asset accumulation are at odds with their reported income.

With this market segment these behaviours can be deliberate - or come about through a lack of skill, experience and or resources to appropriately record and report business activities. Profiling is a step in the process of identifying and treating these behaviours and improving small business tax performance.

The shadow economy occurs when businesses deliberately hide income to avoid paying tax. It is also referred to as the hidden or underground economy. The essential characteristic is that transactions go unrecorded and unreported.

MRA activities include businesses:

paying wages cash-in-hand

skimming some or all cash takings

running a part of their normal business activities off-the-books

not reporting the value of goods and services provided in exchange for other goods and services

operating underground, that is, avoiding their tax and superannuation obligations by not registering their business or lodging returns.

We need to fully understand the taxpayer's individual and business behaviour in the context of the compliance model
Opens in a new window
Opens in a new window
 and BISEP factors (business, industry, social, economic and psychological), and profiles capture our understanding and knowledge of taxpayers and their operating environment to assist with:

Identifying the tax risks we need to mitigate including risk mitigation unable to be achieved through means other than manual intervention, preventing unnecessary client contact.

Supporting compliance case planning and provide the evidence to support our risk recommendations, which ultimately influences the compliance strategies we undertake with the taxpayer.

The purpose of this job aid is to provide information to understand the construction and analysis of profiles to identify potential risks and issues to be addressed within an audit or review, or discount client risks. It is a record of a descriptive summary of the client.

You will establish the audit focus and identify what we know about a client and what we don't know the omissions in the client's picture or anomalies such as gaps in the client profile:

variances in reported income figures

shortfalls in income to meet lifestyle

business performance differs to that of competitors.

This job aid is to be used in conjunction with the relevant case context document (CCD), corporate framework procedure and risk guide. You may also refer to CEG - Profiling a client guide
Opens in a new window
 for base level checks to be undertaken, search tools and systems.

If you need assistance with profiling, seek support from your team leadership.

Profiling

The technical process of profiling involves:

Risk modelling: The profiling process commences with analytical modelling to identify generic risk factors selected for case creation.

Data collection: The population of client profiles and client data from internal and external sources.

Data analysis: Analytical processing including comparative analysis of the data collected and risk identification or mitigation and providing commentary on that analysis to support decision making.

The application of profiling findings is then the next step through planning to address the identified risks.

