Relationships

Taxpayer profiling should combine a range of information that provides a 'whole of taxpayer' view. Identifying the entity structure involves identifying the associates and the family unit of the taxpayer. Identifying all available sources of income and expenses through associated entities provides a complete picture of the taxpayer's income structure.

During the profiling process you will gather more information about the taxpayer and determine which entities in the group should be the focus of the review, for example, the main entity, the trading company, associated trusts, directors.

The profile needs to establish:

What relationships the taxpayer has with other entities?

How could these relationships impact on your audit (source of income/funding)?

What other sources of income from associated entities is available to support personal living expenses (PLE) or unreported income?

The 'Group' icon above the landing of client profile displays the Group Ids relevant to the client. You can select the relevant economic group to display the listed associations/relationships. This lists all associates advised to the ATO as being related to the entity being profiled. It is common for ATO systems not to be updated by taxpayers. MASCOT searches should be completed to ensure all information on the entity and associates are obtained and up to date and includes directors, public officers and shareholders.

To establish the family unit consider the following sources:

spouse details and dependant details on tax returns

health fund coverage data (from COEAS)

common directorships, trustee roles, memberships in self-managed super funds (SMSF), beneficiaries from family trusts ('Relationships' in CCV, MASCOT, trust tax returns, Client links in ICP, etc)

common addresses (tax returns)

external searches (such as Centrelink).

The analysis will also involve compiling a list of entities included in the economic group. This may include entities with and without a tax file number, non-corporate entities, public and private taxpayers and non-residents. Depending on the complexity, a diagram or chart may be appropriate to provide a visual depiction of the structure.

To establish the economic unit complete the following searches:


Relationships link in the Siebel CCV screen

MASCOT searches (both organisational, personal, historical and relational)

holding company information in tax returns.

Where an economic group is identified review the group for trends/consistencies across entities, for example, all reporting losses, performing outside benchmarks, no or low wages.

For complex structures and/or grouped entities consider using the 'Show Groups' icon in ECP to build a visualisation of the client structure.

Note: some sources of data analysed throughout the profile may be matched to entities within the group. For example, a company motor vehicle may have been registered by a director/in the director's name. There is also the potential for changing business structures that may show connections, for example, a sole trader may begin operating their enterprise under a company structure.

