Period of review

You should identify the period of review (POR) and the expiry date during the early stages of profiling. This will help you to ensure any lodgments in focus are within the legislative timeframe for amendments should you proceed with an audit.
Different legislative timeframes for amendments apply depending on the type of lodgment:

Income tax assessments

	

Income tax assessments can be subject to either a 2-year or 4-year POR depending on the income year and complexity of the taxpayer’s entity structure, tax affairs or international dealings.

Refer to Job aid - Determining period of review for tax assessments
Opens in a new window

Note: you must discuss the circumstances of your case and seek approval from your team leader before relying on a 4-year POR for income tax. See IT, GST and Excise Referrals and Escalations
Opens in a new window
 for guidance.




Indirect tax

	

The POR for indirect tax starts on the day on which the Commissioner gives notice of the assessment and ends 4 years starting from the day after the day the assessment is given.

Refer to Requests for extension to the period of review for indirect tax
