.

 
Business activity statements

Complete business activity statement (BAS) analysis using the BAS Analyser.

BAS FY Sum

The BAS FY Sum profile displays the totals for all BAS labels for each financial year the taxpayer has lodged a BAS. This profile can be used to:

provide a snapshot of the total values reported in BAS labels for the year

understand the business results from BAS labels for a period of time

comparison of BAS reported data on a yearly basis.

BAS lodgment history

The BAS Lodgment History profile provides a record of the BAS lodgment from all the members in the taxpayer. This assists to:

identify lodgment issues relating to BAS

understand the BAS lodgment compliance of the taxpayer

confirm lodgment.

BAS details

The BAS Details profile provides details of the latest assessment of all BAS lodged by the client. It also provides a row displaying financial year totals.

Comparative analysis should be completed across lodgment periods and financial years to identify any anomalies. The client's BAS data needs to be examined to identify trends, exceptions and lodgment patterns.

When evaluating the BAS Analyser concentrate initially on reviewing the BAS Summary details. You should note any areas of risk that may require investigation. For example:

negative cash flow
the cumulative total for 1B is greater than 1A
the cumulative total for G11 is greater than G1
Note: Simpler BAS does not report G11/G10
an unusual, unexplained transaction reported on a previous BAS.

More detailed analysis should then follow by analysing the following information sourced from the BAS Analyser including:

1A (GST collected): G1 (Total Supplies)

If all the supplies that an entity makes are taxable, the ratio of 1A:G1 will be 9.09% (1/11th) if the client has reported the GST inclusive amount at G1. Industry knowledge will also assist to identify anomalies where mixed supplies are made.

Deviation from this base figure will indicate the extent of GST-free, input-taxed or exported supplies.

1B (GST paid): the sum of G10 and G11 (Capital and other acquisitions) other than simpler BAS

Deviation from this base figure will indicate the extent of non-creditable acquisitions. This could indicate whether the entity is reporting all acquisitions. Where 1B is 1/11th the risk of over claiming ITCs should be considered.

If all of the acquisitions that an entity makes are creditable, the ratio of 1B:G10 + G11 will be 9.09% (1/11th) if the client has reported the GST inclusive amount.

G2 (Export sales): G1 (Total Supplies)

This ratio will give the percentage of all supplies that are exported.

Variation in this figure will indicate whether exports have become a greater or smaller part of the total supplies made by the entity.

G3 (GST-Free): G1 (Total Supplies)

This ratio will give the percentage of all supplies that are GST-free.

Variation in this figure will indicate whether GST-free supplies have become a greater or smaller part of the total supplies made by the entity. Increases in the level of GST-free supplies could present a risk of misclassification of supplies.

1A 1B (Net GST Liability)

If the calculation is greater than 0 - the entity has collected more GST in the tax period than they have paid.

If the calculation is less than 0 - the entity has paid more GST in the tax period than they have collected.

Variations will indicate whether an entity regularly receives refunds or if it is more common for the client to have a GST liability and assists in identifying entities that have claimed a GST refund even though the net amount on the BAS is a debit.

