Refreshed period of review

When a return is amended, the period of review is refreshed, but only for the particular or particulars (that is, the labels) that were adjusted. Therefore, multiple periods of review may be applicable to the one assessment.

If there is a prior amendment for the period, you must assess if the period of review has been refreshed for the label (or labels) being adjusted on this amendment. The steps below will guide you.

There are limitations on the types of amendments the Commissioner can make to a label within its refreshed period of review. See subsection 170(3)
Opens in a new window
 of the ITAA 1936.

Generally, you can only amend the earlier assessment on a particular label within its refreshed period of review in a way that decreases the taxpayer's liability. The Commissioner may amend the later assessment on that particular label in a way that increases the taxpayer's liability if the:

earlier amendment was to reduce the taxpayer liability, and
Commissioner accepted a statement made by the taxpayer in making that earlier amendment.
Step	Action


1

	

Check if the period has previously been amended. If:

yes, go to step 2
no, use the effective date of the original Notice of Assessment (NOA) to calculate the period of review.



2

	

Collate and compare this amendment with the original assessment and all previous amendments, using the ICP Form Capture Macro.
Opens in a new window




3

	

Note the fields that were adjusted on this and all previous amendments.




4

	

Check if the same field or fields that are being adjusted on this amendment were adjusted on a previous amendment. For any fields that:

were previously adjusted, for that field only, use the effective date of the Notice of Amended Assessment (NOAA) that resulted from the amendment where that field was previously adjusted
were not previously adjusted, use the effective date of the original NOA.

