Refund Imputation Credits (RIC) or Refund Franking Credits (RFC)

For the financial years 2001/2002 to 2012/2013, POR applies to amendments to the dividends. ICP does not allow an adjustment to franking credits to occur without an amendment of the dividends, so although franking credits for the period did not form part of the assessment (and are not then subject to POR), all requests must be processed as amendments. An objection and request for extension of time must be received.

It does not matter whether original and subsequent forms are short forms or full ITRs, short forms are processed as assessments and an NOA/NOAA should issue. Treat the original form as an original assessment (producing an NOA) and any subsequent form as an amendment (producing an NOAA).

For the 2012-13 income year and prior

Ultimately, whether POR applies to an adjustment or amendment of a return for a year before the 2013-14 income year is dependent on the facts. The following discussion of adjusting a credit versus amending a particular year of the assessment, e.g. income, explains why this is so.

For the 2003-04 income year and prior, subsection 6(1) of ITAA 1936 provided that 'assessment' means:

(a) The ascertainment of:

(i) The amount of taxable income

(ii) ....etc.

And of the tax payable on that taxable income or net income.

For the 2004-05 to 2012-13 income years inclusive, subsection 6(1) provided that 'assessment' means:

(a) The ascertainment of the amount of taxable income (or that there is no taxable income) and of the tax payable on that income (or that no tax is payable).

Whether a POR applies to an RIC or RFC amendment request will depend on what the amendment is proposing to adjust.

If:

the taxpayer is requesting an adjustment to the imputation or franking credit amount only then this can be actioned outside of the POR – the entitlement to the credit is separate from the assessment process and the credit does not form part of the assessment at law.
however, the taxpayer is seeking to adjust the amount of the reported dividend/s or both dividends and the credit amount then the adjustment to the dividend amount/s is subject to POR (the dividends form part of taxable income which is part of the assessment as defined at law).

Note: There are limitations in ICP (known issues) that currently restrict the ability to 'adjust' a credit outside of the assessment process. The only way to bring about this adjustment is to process an amendment through ICP – technically if the only change is to a credit amount this is not part of the assessment and cannot form part of an amendment at law even though this is the only way we currently have to process the changes.

A tax return lodged should be treated as an amendment request where a stand-alone form has already been lodged. What is to be 'adjusted' will check whether it is an amendment at law and whether POR should apply or not.

"If a taxpayer lodged an income tax return and then lodged a stand-alone RIC or RFC in the above circumstances, would this be allowed?"

This should be treated as an amendment request and what is to be 'adjusted' will check whether it is an amendment at law and whether POR should apply or not.

"ICP system treats RIC and RFC stand-alones as income tax assessments. This can then affect the true NOAA date, which is a date that we use to check a period of review. Can you please advise how these are to be treated?"

Where there is an original lodgment (whether short form or otherwise) any further lodgment should be treated as an amendment request and what is to be 'adjusted' will check if it is an amendment at law and whether POR should apply or not. If relevant the POR should start from the NOA received date as these are valid assessments.

For the 2013-14 and later income years

From 1 July 2013 and after, the definition of 'assessment' in 6(1) of the ITAA 1936 is amended to include:

(a) the ascertainment:

(i) of the amount of taxable income (or that there is no taxable income); and

(ii) of the tax payable on that taxable income (or that no tax is payable); and

(iii) of the total of a taxpayer's tax offset refunds for a year of income (or that the taxpayer can get no such refunds for the year of income);

For assessments made on or after 1 July 2013 for the 2013-14 income year or later income years, the ascertainment of the total of a taxpayer's tax offset refunds (including franking credit) for a year of income is part of an assessment, therefore:

the amount of a tax offset can only be changed through the amendment of an assessment and must therefore occur within the time limits for amending an assessment, and
the periods of review for assessments apply.
