ATO view on Repayment of Income and Beneficial Entitlement

The following information is a summary of the current ATO view in respect to the repayment of overpaid salary/wages and/or taxable government pensions or allowances where the income has been included as assessable income in prior income years.

The Application of Section 59-30 

Section 59-30 of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (ITAA 1997) applies so that an amount a taxpayer receives is not assessable income and is not exempt income for an income year if the:

taxpayer must repay it; and
amount is repaid in a later income year; and
taxpayer cannot deduct the repayment for any income year

This provision will apply, for example, where a taxpayer receives an amount from their employer, but due to an event (i.e. not meeting a condition of employment) is required to repay this amount in the later income year.

In such cases, the taxpayer can seek an amendment for the year in which the income was originally included to reduce the amount of their assessable income.

Subsection 170(10AA) of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 (ITAA 1936) provides that the limited statutory periods outlined in section 170 of the ITAA 1936 do not apply to give effect to section 59-30 of the ITAA 1997. This means that an Extension of Time (EoT) request is not required for an amendment to be processed, however documentary evidence that the income has been repaid must be supplied with the amendment request.

Section 59-30 of the ITAA 1997 does not apply in instances where a taxpayer is required to repay an amount as a result of an error or mistake (for example, an overpayment of a taxable government pension, allowance or payment, or an overpayment of salary that must subsequently be repaid).

Taxation determination 2008/9
Entitlement
Taxation Determination 2008/9

Taxation Determination 2008/9
Opens in a new window
 identifies that amounts incorrectly paid as salary and wages, or income support payments which have been paid to a person who is not beneficially entitled to that payment, are not deemed to be payments of income according to ordinary concepts, and as such are not regarded as assessable income under section 6-5 of the ITAA 1997.

The essence of TD 2008/09 is that there is a distinction between amendments in relation to repayments of income which a taxpayer was entitled to receive at the time, and income which they were never entitled to receive. The rationale underlying this distinction is that, if the taxpayer was never entitled to receive the payment, they never had beneficial entitlement to the income and thus did not derive the income. 

Where a taxpayer is required to repay an amount which has been included as assessable income in a prior income year, the taxpayer can seek an amendment for the year in which the income was originally included to reduce the amount of the assessable income. To reduce the assessable income, a determination needs to be made as to whether or not the taxpayer was entitled to the payment when they received it.

Entitlement

Where the taxpayer was entitled to the income and was correctly paid an amount, but subsequent events resulted in the requirement to repay that income (for example, the taxpayer receives a payment on condition that they will remain with an employer for a specified period but because the taxpayer does not meet the condition they are required to repay the employer) an amendment can be made at any time provided the amount has been repaid i.e. where there was entitlement to the income but subsequent events require the repayment of that income, and that repayment has been made, an extension of time request is not required for an amendment to be processed, however, documentary evidence that the income has been repaid must be supplied with the amendment request.

Where the taxpayer was not entitled to the income, and was paid that amount due to an error or mistake (for example: an overpayment of a taxable government pension, allowance or payment, or an overpayment of salary and wages that must subsequently be repaid) the overpaid amount does not have to be repaid before an amendment can be allowed.

An extension of time request would normally be required where the applicable period of review has expired. However, for overpayments of a taxable government pension, allowance or payment, an extension of time request is not required as the amendment request can be actioned as if it had been received within time.

Exclusion – Lump Sum payment

An amendment will not be allowed where a taxpayer is required to repay an amount of a benefit or regularly paid compensation payment due to the subsequent receipt of a lump sum compensation payment or lump sum payment for damages for a wrong or injury suffered as a result of the taxpayer's occupation. For example, where wages are paid to a taxpayer by an insurer while sick or injured, and that amount is recouped by the insurer from a lump sum calculated on settlement of the claim, the amount 'recouped' by the insurer cannot be used to reduce the assessable income of the taxpayer in any prior year where that income was originally claimed as assessable income.

Appendix A
Tax treatment of Out of Time amendments for entities with Carried forward losses, Deferred losses and Capital losses

Advice was obtained from the Business and Personal Taxes Centre of Expertise, Law and Practice on the following scenario:

A taxpayer (individual or Company) lodges amendment requests for the 2007 to 2010 income year tax assessments. The taxpayer requests an amendment to include a loss of $1M for the 2007 income year (original assessed taxable income was $100,000). The taxpayer also requests an amendment of the 2008 assessment to take account of a carried forward loss of $1M with the result that the taxable income for that income year is recalculated as nil. The original assessment for the 2008 income year is based on a taxable income of $200,000. Neither the 2007 nor the 2008 assessment can be amended as they are outside the applicable time limit of 2 years prescribed under section 170 of the Income Tax Assessment Act 1936 (ITAA 1936).

The taxpayer also claims a loss of $ 800,000 is available to carry forward from the 2008 income year – firstly to the 2009 income year and then to the 2010 income year – being the respective balances remaining of the $1M loss incurred in the 2007 income year. The 2009 and 2010 income year assessments are still within the amendment period and the taxpayer requests that both assessments be amended despite the facts that the two prior year assessments cannot be amended and an assessment has issued for the 2009 income year.

For the purposes of this scenario, the possibility of the taxpayer successfully requesting an extension of time to object to the 2007 and 2008 assessments was ignored. It was also assumed that:

the taxpayer is a self-assessment taxpayer and has no exempt income;
none of the conditions in subsections 170(5) to 170(7) of the ITAA 1936 are satisfied;
the taxpayer's affairs are not so complex that the four-year amendment period applies; and
the assessment for the 2009 income year is not outside the 2-year prescribed period for amendment.
Questions
Can the assessed taxable incomes of the 2007 and/or the 2008 income years be recalculated – as opposed to amended – to take into account the tax loss incurred in the 2007 income year for the purposes of calculating the taxable income of later income years even though actual amendment of those assessments is precluded by the time limits imposed by section 170 of the ITAA 1936?
If the answer to question (1) is yes, is that tax loss available to be carried forward to the 2009 and 2010 income years under the current law?
Advice
Yes, a tax loss may be recalculated for income years where the amendment period is outside the time limits imposed by section 170 of the ITAA 1936
As recalculated, any remaining tax loss is available to be carried forward to later income years. The fact that a subsequent intervening income year has been assessed on a taxable income does not change the outcome of whether or not that assessment is out of time for amendment – see subsections 36-15(1) and 36-15(2) How to deduct tax losses of entities other than corporate tax.

The same reasoning applies to cases where there is a recalculation of a net capital loss under section 102-10 as the calculation of a net capital loss does not form part of the making of an assessment under section 166 of the ITAA 1936.

In the case referred to, it was decided that the prohibition of any amendment by section 170 did not prejudice the taxpayer's entitlement to section 80(2) deductions aggregating $17,000 in subsequent years.

Summary

In dealing with similar cases in the future, the principle to be followed is that the deduction allowable under section 80(2) in respect of a prior year's loss is available if, in fact, such a loss has been incurred and remains unrecouped. In other words, the availability of the deduction is not affected by the fact that the provisions of section 170 preclude the earlier year's assessment being reopened to convert an assessed figure of taxable income into a loss.

Correspondingly, a prohibition against the amendment of a prior year's assessment should not be treated as a barrier against the use of the correct amount of taxable income (i.e., assessable income less allowable deductions) for the year concerned in calculating the average income of a primary producer for a later year.

(5) These rulings are based on the view that the raising of an assessment for a particular year requires, independently of anything that has occurred in relation to any other year's assessment, a proper application of every provision of the legislation which affects the determination of the amounts of taxable income and tax payable for that year.

