Introduction

This page is designed to support Compliance and Engagement officers conducting profiling of a taxpayer from base level checks and the systems available.

In conducting a case you should develop an understanding of the taxpayer's background, industry, compliance behaviour and the environment in which they operate. This is developed through:

profiling
taxpayer interview, interaction and observation
case verification and testing action.

Profiling generally involves researching information held in ATO systems and a range of other sources of information to develop an understanding of the taxpayer. While profiling will largely be completed in the Plan phase of a case, actions conducted during the Execute phase are likely to enhance your understanding of the taxpayer and should be included in your taxpayer profile.

Your understanding of the taxpayer will assist you to:

assess the individual and business behaviour of the taxpayer
provide some context around the compliance action
assess their level of compliance and position on the compliance model
Opens in a new window
identify compliance risks
determine the most appropriate treatments (testing/methods etc) to address identified risks.

The Information Hub SharePoint
Opens in a new window
 provides access to the A-Z Database listing, company and industry research that may support profiling activities.

How much profiling should I conduct?

The extent of profiling required in each case will depend on requirements outlined in the Case Context Document (CCD), project documentation, particular case product used and the identified risks. As a general rule, you should complete the base level checks outlined below prior to contacting the taxpayer.

Refer to your business line procedures for a profiling guide appropriate to your market segment. In some instances, the taxpayer profile may be completed using automation technology.
Base level checks

Prior to commencing your profiling ensure you understand the reasons for case selection.

To obtain a general understanding on the taxpayer, navigate to the Siebel Client Consolidated View (CCV)
Opens in a new window
 and:

review Siebel notes and consider any notes that may impact your case
check Siebel activities and determine whether they have any impact on your case and where appropriate, contact the actioning officer to discuss further
check for any Siebel alerts or indicators i.e. Non-resident, High Wealth Individual (HWI)
Opens in a new window
, or Lodgment Program (on Tax Practitioner Consolidated View (TPCV)) which indicates the agent's current circumstances regarding their lodgment program
consider if there are vulnerability indicators and support required. Refer to Vulnerability Framework
Opens in a new window
 (myATO) and Tailored support for taxpayers experiencing vulnerability
Opens in a new window
check for active cases and consider the type of case, area actioning the case and determine whether it may have any impact on how you proceed with your case. Check if the client associated with your case has a client manager
Opens in a new window
check for closed cases and if relevant, review case notes, finalisation report and other attachments
view related cases details and consider any impact this may have on the scope of your case and any potential escalation to another product
view any service requests and consider any impact this may have on the scope of your case
view all correspondence and details, and consider any impact this may have on the scope of your case i.e. referral to external collection agency
confirm if taxpayer has been referred to an External collection agency (ECA) by reviewing Confirmation of external collection agency referral
Opens in a new window
. If the case is required to be withdrawn, proceed to Withdraw the case
Opens in a new window
.

Your taxpayer profile should include a summary of the following:

current registration information (including current system indicators)
previous and current ATO contact
lodgment history (lodgment dates, lodgment mediums and any details indicating who has lodged the return). Note: these details can be indicators of whether an exemption for imposition of administrative penalty (known as 'safe harbour') may need to be considered
check if the taxpayer is using the Simpler BAS (in Instalment Processing System (IPS), on the Report Method list screen, 'Simpler' will be displayed in the Reporting column if the Business Activity Statement is a simple BAS)
debt history and upcoming due dates for lodgments and payments
business information (e.g. industry norms, information from the taxpayer's web site and industry web sites)
reporting trends (BAS analysis, BAS/IT comparisons).

