Tracing taxpayers

Assists to trace taxpayers where it has not been possible to contact them via the recorded contact details

Last published 11 November 2024

Favourite Notes 

The electronic version of this document is the only authorised version.
Printed copies may be out of date.

Overview

This guide assists compliance officers trace taxpayers where it has not been possible to contact them via the recorded contact details.

Before commencing tracing action, ensure you have attempted to contact the authorised representative, allowing for circumstances where the taxpayer's, or their representative's, office may be briefly unattended.  Consider the information in Primary contact and authorised contacts
Opens in a new window
. 

You should also have sent the initial client contact letter to the taxpayer's preferred mailing address requesting the client make contact with you.

Tracing actions have been categorised into 2 levels:

Level 1 tracing may be conducted where initial attempts to contact the authorised representative are unsuccessful.
Level 2 tracing may be conducted where Level 1 tracing is unsuccessful and with team leader approval.
Level 1 tracing

Where messages have been left with the client and no response has been received, you should apply the following sequence in order to trace the taxpayer  If you require assistance, discuss with our team leader and/or an information gathering adviser.
Opens in a new window

Ensure you consider Privacy, taxpayer and registry confidentiality CEI
Opens in a new window
 and Information disclosure CEI
Opens in a new window
.

1.  Conduct system searches.

Conduct searches using ATO systems and other systems such as the AUSTRAC to trace the taxpayer.  For further guidance on system searches refer to the enterprise-wide and your business line's profiling procedures.

2.  Contact the taxpayer's tax agent.

If contact has not been established with the authorised representative, or a new arrangement confirmed, contact the taxpayer's tax agent for assistance.

3.  Contact the taxpayer's place of business.

You could consider contacting the taxpayer at their place of business if the authorised representative fails to respond to your letter.  This should be discussed with your team leader prior to contacting.

If you contact the taxpayer's place of business by telephone:

ask to speak to the public officer / office manager / director / partner
explain the difficulty you have experienced in contacting the authorised representative
if necessary, enquire whether an alternative authorised representative is available, and
obtain written confirmation of any new arrangement from the taxpayer prior to proceeding with the case.

4.  Prepare and send the appropriate uncontactables letter to the authorised representative using the recorded address details.

If your tracing establishes new contact details

Attempt to contact the taxpayer by phone or issue the initial client contact letter to the new address.

If your tracing efforts are successful in establishing contact with the taxpayer

confirm that the new contact details are to be used for future contact
update taxpayer contact details on ATO systems, and
continue with the compliance action.

However, if you do not receive a response or contact is not made using the new contact details prepare and send the appropriate 'uncontactables' letter to the authorised representative at the new address. If appropriate, insert the paragraph where the taxpayer has failed to respond to a previous letter sent requesting contact.

You should discuss your options with your team leader and/or an information gathering adviser
Opens in a new window
 within your business line if a response is not received to the 'uncontactables' letter.  Considerations should include:

a progression to Level 2 tracing (if not already conducted), and 
your business line guidance on actioning cases where the taxpayer is not contactable.
If your tracing does not establish new contact details

Discuss your options with your team leader and/or an information gathering adviser 
Opens in a new window
 within your business line.

Your considerations should include:

a progression to Level 2 tracing (if not already conducted) and

your business line guidance on actioning cases where the taxpayer is not contactable.
Level 2 tracing

Ensure you obtain team leader approval to conduct any Level 2 tracing actions.  This is recorded in a Siebel approval activity:

Load an additional activity
Opens in a new window
Assign an activity to another case user
Opens in a new window

Level 2 tracing actions include the following.  You should contact an Information Gathering adviser to discuss these further  Ensure you consider the Privacy, taxpayer and registry confidentiality register CEI
Opens in a new window
 and Information disclosure CEI.
Opens in a new window

Visiting the taxpayer's premises.

If contact has not been established during the visit, consider other options such as contacting:

any new tenants of the business premises
neighbouring businesses, and
landlords or letting agencies.
Conducting third party enquiries.

You may need to conduct third party enquiries in order to obtain details of the taxpayer's current address or whereabouts.

You should follow corporate guidance on making third party enquiries
Opens in a new window
, particularly the section on obtaining details of the taxpayers current address or whereabouts. Be aware that third party enquiries may require the use of information gathering powers.

