Digital ID Fraud - myID/RAM

Escalate to AGDIS fraud referral team

If the unauthorised third-party lodgment (for example, revised BAS) was lodged via Online Services for Business (OSfB)
Opens in a new window
, it may have been accessed using a fraudulent myID.

myID is a unique digital identity used to log in to government online services, including OSB. It is different from myGov and can be likened to the 100-point ID check, but on a smart device. To access online services on behalf of a business or entity, a taxpayer will need to link their myID to the business using Relationship Authorisation Manager (RAM).

Check the following systems to determine the lodgment channel of the unauthorised third-party lodgment:

ECP	Identify gadget BAS Lodgment and drill down the link to identify the lodgment source e.g. tax agent, ATO Online. 
RAPT 	 Select BAS Lodgment History; the lodgment source will be shown as Business Portal.

Refer to Activity statement lodgment mediums
Opens in a new window
 for further information.

Escalation point:

When is a referral for suspected or confirmed to Digital Identity fraud required?

For clients in business - if the lodgment was via OSfB

If lodgments via OSB have been identified and are suspected or confirmed to be fraudulent, this indicates that an individual, an associate or additional individuals have been linked to the business in the RAM with a Digital Identity (myID).

For an individual to log in to OSB they must have a Digital Identity (myID) and be linked in RAM. If an individual/associate and the business has been linked in RAM and there is reasonable suspicion of Digital Identity fraud:

complete the AGDIS Digital Identity Fraud referral template
Opens in a new window
 and
escalate to the AGDIS_fraud_referral@ato.gov.au for investigation.

Note: if you have spoken to an individual, associate or representative of a business and fraud has been confirmed, once the referral form is submitted, advise the taxpayer they may receive a call from the myID/RAM fraud investigations team.

The AGDIS Triage Centre at the ATO will lodge an incident through to the Australian Government Digital Identity System (AGDIS) System Administrator.

The AGDIS System Administrator will pass through and disclose relevant information to the Whole of Government Digital Identity (myID/RAM) fraud team to investigate whether a fraudulent myID has created in the individual/associates name, and whether the individual/associate has been linked in RAM.

The Whole of Government Digital Identity (myID/RAM) fraud team will take appropriate action based on their findings. This includes:

Investigating and suspending any fraudulent myID credentials preventing further access to OSB via that myID.
Locking any compromised identity documents to prevent use to create a myID.
Deactivate any fraudulently created links in RAM as an additional security measure.
respond back to the AGDIS System Administrator with the findings and confirmation of action taken.

The AGDIS System Administrator will respond back to the AGDIS Triage Centre at the ATO with the outcome.

 
