Types of formal approaches:

Our formal notice powers (under section 353-10 of Schedule 1 to Taxation Administration Act 1953 (TAA))

We use our formal notice powers by serving a notice on the taxpayer or other entity that sets out:

information to be given

documents to be supplied

if a person is required to attend an interview with us and provide information and evidence.

We can also serve a notice that includes a combination of some of the above requirements.

Our formal access powers (under section 353-15 of Schedule 1 to the TAA 1953):

When using our access powers, we are authorised to enter and remain on any land, premises or place and have full and free access to books, documents (including electronic documents) goods or other property. We also have the ability to test and analyse goods and other property and to take samples for indirect tax and excise law purposes.

We are required to show our wallet authority when taking 'access'.

The occupier of the land, premises or place has a legal obligation to provide reasonable facilities and assistance including answer questions in relation to undertaking the access, such as advising where tax records are located, providing computer passwords, etc. However, the occupier is not compelled to answer any questions that do not relate to the access activities.

When to use:

We prefer to work co-operatively with taxpayers to obtain information we require and will generally consult with taxpayers before using our formal powers. If we cannot obtain the information in a co-operative way, we may use our formal powers of information gathering or access.

We use this formal approach for example, where:

The information we have been given is not complete and accurate.

There has been a lack of a cooperation or concern that fraud or evasion is involved.

We require information from third parties and the information is subject to confidentiality arrangements.

The taxpayer has requested a formal approach, for example, to protect them from breaching privacy obligations.

We may also use our formal powers at the outset in certain circumstances.

