Considerations for using E-Audit in your active compliance case

Last published 14 January 2026

Favourite Notes Collapse all 

You should engage and seek input from the E-Audit team early in your case, preferably before making a request for information (RFI). Following advice from the E-Audit analyst will help you to maximise the benefits of using E-Audit.

E-Audit is most useful in cases involving taxpayers using computerised systems to collate large numbers of transactions. E-Audit allows for analysis of larger amounts of data than would be possible by manual review.

E-Audit is used generally for substantive testing; that is if there is a need to determine if items are in error, or a problem needs to be quantified, or certain items identified.

Substantive procedures provide evidence that reveals monetary misstatements in the recording or reporting of transactions and balances. Types of substantive procedures are:

analytical procedures.

tests of details of transactions.

tests of details of balances.

 
Info Alert
Note

If you're conducting a Private Wealth (PW) case not part of the GST or Shadow Economy programs, contact the Private Wealth Data Team
Opens in a new window
 for assistance.

Before using E-Audit as part of an active compliance case, you should consider relevant information on this page.

Considerations for using E-Audit

You should consider using E-Audit in active compliance cases where you find the taxpayer has:

a computerised accounting system

data is obtainable

there are a large number of transactions

Warning Alert
Note

If you acquire bulk third party data which will later undergo computerised comparison against ATO records, you should also observe the obligations contained in Data matching - special purpose acquisition dat SEI 
Opens in a new window

E-Audit analysts are well-placed to offer a wider range of services than simple number-crunching. You should seek the assistance of an E-Audit analyst if your case involves:
Electronic ecosystems

Accounting software.

Excel spreadsheets.

Point of Sale (POS).

Cash register data.

Electronic bank transactional data.

BPAY facilities.

Online sales and purchases.

Cloud data.

Data analysis and visualisation

Timing differences that might provide tax advantages.

Expected high numbers of exceptions.

Problems associating reporting items with the specified risk.

Value that can be added by examining more transactions.

Displaying data in different ways.

Identifying unusual or strange items.

Data validation and comparison

Third-party data.

Pay As You Go Withholding records.

Superannuation records.

Testing the validity of items by crosschecking them with other information.

Validating the accuracy and integrity of the data.

Validating calculations.

Record keeping issues

Non-standard or obviously deficient business systems.

Difficulties in obtaining taxpayers electronic records.

Testing integrity of electronic business systems.

Specific use cases and efficiencies

Reducing time to conduct compliance activities by utilising technology.

Increasing compliance coverage by analysing the whole population rather than a sample of the population.

Strengthening audit findings through the use of analytical forensic data.

Factors which will affect your decision to use E-Audit

Case objectives.

Size of files.

Depth of data.

Feasibility/cost of downloading data.

Case key dates and deadlines.

How to proceed

If you decide that E-Audit is appropriate for your case, create a referral activity, as outlined in Request E-Audit assistance
