PG Compliance audit management plan guide

To support and facilitate open relationships with taxpayers we use the audit management plan (AMP).This document provides information and guidance on: when and why you use the AMP, completing the AMP template, and preparing for the initial meeting, where the AMP will be discussed.

Last published 19 June 2023

Favourite Notes 

The electronic version of this document is the only authorised version.
Printed copies may be out of date.

Introduction


To support and facilitate open relationships with taxpayers we use the audit management plan (AMP).


This document provides information and guidance on:

when and why you use the AMP
completing the AMP template, and
preparing for the initial meeting, where the AMP will be discussed.

Background

Large businesses have ongoing relationships with:

Public Groups & Internationals (PG&I) and Private Groups and High Wealth individuals (PGH) for income tax, and
Public Groups Engagement (PGE) Compliance GST.

Using an AMP will improve, support and facilitate open relationships with taxpayers, and ensure large business taxpayers receive consistency, regardless of which part of the compliance program they are dealing with.

Purpose

The AMP is used for the planning and ongoing management of an audit on a cooperative basis with the taxpayer. Using the AMP:

provides a framework for the initial meeting with the taxpayer
encourages regular and open relationships
allows you to share an understanding of the audit process and obtain commitments from the taxpayer, and
facilitates meeting key milestones and completion of the audit when used at regular intervals during the audit.

The AMP allows us to clearly explain:

the scope of the audit
the stages of the audit
what we will do if we find new issues to pursue
the protocols for obtaining information including protocols for Legal professional privilege (LPP) and Accountants' Concession, and
the protocols for what we will do if information is not provided within agreed timeframes.
Process

The following provides an overview of the process in using the AMP to assist with the planning and ongoing management of the audit:

 

Step

	

Process




1

	

Prepare the initial AMP using the AMP template.

Note: You may not be able to complete all sections of the AMP prior to the initial meeting.





2

	

At the initial taxpayer interview:

provide a copy of the initial AMP
commit to providing regular updates (at least monthly) on the audit progress and to communicate anything that affects your progress
set expected due dates for the different stages of the audit
seek to reach agreement on any operational or audit scope issues for the most efficient progression of the audit

advise that information requests will be captured with the Record of information requests log, and a copy will be provided to the taxpayer, together with an updated AMP

advise how you plan to report your audit findings (consider audit scope and whether you will provide a separate report for each risk or report on the audit as a whole in one report)

advise the taxpayer of the position paper process, and


the availability of an Independent Review of the statement of audit position.




3

	

At the end of the initial meeting, you and the taxpayer should have a clear understanding and agreement of the next phase of the audit.




4

	

Update the AMP to reflect your initial interview.




5

	

Revisit and update the AMP at regular intervals.




6

	

Update the AMP to reflect any discussions and outcomes from mandatory active case management activities, such as the monthly reviews.




7

	

Provide the taxpayer with the AMP whenever updated.

Naming convention


When adding an attachment at the Case or Activity level in Siebel, you are required to use the CEG naming convention guidelines as follows:


(reverse date)_(description)_(sub-description, if required)_(name of party, where appropriate)_(topic)_(version number)[include for updated versions only]

The approved naming convention allows for clear identification of the attachment, so that a third party reviewer can easily locate and identify the nature of the attachment. It allows for better attachment management and version control of documents and is in keeping with the record keeping practice statement Records Management- CEI 2014/01/01.

You should use plain English for the description and include the version for documents with the same descriptor for example:

Audit management plan_taxpayer name_V1

Audit management plan_taxpayer name_V2

Attachments can be sorted by name in the Attachments view. You should use words that clearly describe the document at the start of the attachments name to assist with sorting. Siebel will record the date that you attached the document.

Siebel management

The following action is required to manage the AMP within Siebel case from end to end:

 

Step

	

Description




1

	

Create the template, name it in accordance with the naming convention, and attach to the case.




2

	

Complete the fields in the AMP.

Note: perceived risks described in the issue description field of the Siebel issues view, should align with the risks listed in the AMP.





3

	

Record details of any discussions with your approving officer about the development of the AMP in the notes view.




4

	

Assign the Quality Control activity (with the AMP attached) to your authorising officer for approval.

Note: this is the initial AMP developed prior to discussions with the taxpayer and likely to be updated following your meeting with the taxpayer.




5

	

The approving officer approves your plan and publishes the AMP to be provided to the taxpayer and discussed at the initial interview.




6

	

If the audit scope changes go to Step 7

Otherwise go to step 9




7

	

When the audit scope is changed:

open the most recent published version of the AMP you created in step 1

save a copy of this document using the appropriate naming convention.

Note: audit scope changes that require approval are:

changes to the period to be examined (period extended where material risks are identified), and
changes, additions or removal of risks.



8

	

Obtain approval for audit scope changes from your approving officer.




9

	

When minor AMP updates are made (such as, no audit scope changes eg updating timeframes, contact details) record details in the notes view.




10

	

Provide a copy of updated AMP to the taxpayer and record details in the notes view.

 

 

	

All versions of the AMP provided to the taxpayer must be published. This provides a complete record/log of what we provided to the taxpayer and all changes made.

Other things to consider

Outlined below are some other issues to consider when preparing your audit management plan, and executing your audit.

Where relevant, document any discussion regarding the issues below in the appropriate working papers (for example the audit management plan, the monthly review or the notes view).

Senior officers to attend initial meetings

Appropriate senior officers need to be engaged in your audit case (refer to table below). A senior tax officer must discuss the audit plan with the taxpayer.

 

 

	

Invite the senior officer who will attend this interview to a planning workshop.

The following table details officers must attend the initial audit interview:

 

RDF category of Taxpayer

	

Senior Officer required




Higher risk

	

SES Officer

(Segment/Program SES or Case Leader SES as appropriate to the case/situation)




Key taxpayer

	

SES officer where available, otherwise:

EL2.2 Director
EL2.1 Team leader/Audit leader, or
EL2.1 Technical leader



Medium or lower risk

	

EL2.1 Team leader/Audit leader, or

EL2.1 Technical leader

Note: Only include your director's details in the ATO audit team section of the AMP if the director is actually part of the team conducting the audit.

Your director's details should always be included in the How we will communicate section of the AMP as a possible escalation point. As such, it is preferred that they are not listed as a member of the audit team unless they are actually participating in the audit.

Preliminary audit interview

In preparation for the taxpayer interview, consider the following points for discussion with the taxpayer:

Taxpayer personnel details

Identify the main contact for the audit (you may know this prior to the meeting).
Identify key personnel who will have responsibility for the risks we have identified and make arrangements to interview them (if required).
organise with the taxpayer, all contacts that will allow you to identify and/or design appropriate tests of controls and substantive tests.

Operational issues

Determine if the taxpayer has any contentious views that may affect the audit.
Determine if the taxpayer has undertaken any internal governance reviews and if so, if they are prepared to release the report to us (bear in mind the taxpayer may claim LPP or similar).
Consider any potential blockers that may result from, restructures, staff availability, staff changes and system changes, problems with access to information.

 


 

	

Consider the Operational risk management guidelines.

Perceived compliance risk(s)

Determine from the taxpayer whether there is any additional information that may influence your preliminary assessment of perceived compliance risk(s).
Whether the taxpayer has any issues with the audit scope ie risks identified and period to be examined.
Who the taxpayer representative(s) will be with the responsibility for facilitating your audit tests.

Notifying and updating audit timeframes

In preparation for the taxpayer interview you are required to set planned due dates for the different stages of the audit. It is not acceptable to record the due dates in the AMP as 'to be advised'.

In consultation with the taxpayer at the initial interview you should negotiate the dates you expect the different stages to be completed and record these in the AMP.

During the course of the audit should you need to revise the due dates (after discussion with the taxpayer) record the new date in the AMP and re-issue a copy to the taxpayer.

As all versions of the AMP provided to the taxpayer must be published, you will have a complete record/log of the changes made regarding the due dates.

Information/documentation gathering

Initial delays in providing information may seem to be minor but it is one of the main reasons for exceeding case cycle times.

Formal powers are not the preferred approach to getting information. Their use will only occur in a limited number of cases and generally only after the informal approach has been unsuccessful.

Ongoing constructive dialogue enhances information flows. Discussions with taxpayers need to be regular, timely and candid to keep the compliance process on track to avoid delays in the case.

Taxpayer cooperation is more than meeting timeframes for the provision of information. It includes working together to ensure the efficient and timely progress of the compliance activity through the provision of full, complete and timely information.

Information requirements should be fully discussed with taxpayers at the commencement and during the course of the compliance activity. The initial interview should cover:

the information being sought and the relevance to the risk
their ability to provide information including information held in an electronic format
developing protocols for the supply of information including escalation processes where information needs are not being met
building a common understanding of the level of cooperation expected
agreeing on a process to resolve possible claims for LPP and the accountants concession
discussion about the circumstances where the use of formal powers may occur
gaining an understanding about the nature of records held, who has control, where they are
agreeing on timeframes and consequences of not being met (move to formal powers)

any requests for information is to be captured in the Record of information requests, (provide a copy of this, together with the updated audit management plan (if appropriate) to the taxpayer)

advise that any delays in the provision of requested information will impact your ability to meet the deadlines you have proposed, and
determine
whether your planned testing dates are workable for the taxpayer, and
the location(s) of the testing.

Record of information requests

The record of information requests form provides a means to provide the taxpayer with all details of previous and current information/record requests for the audit case. It provides a summary of:

what has been requested
when it was requested
how the request was made ( that is, verbal, letter (informal) or under access powers (formal)
who has made the request
the agreed due date
was the request for information complied with in full
the status of the request (that is, complete, or overdue by number of days, or due now, or due in number of days).
Expectations

We expect that a large business audit will be concluded within two years of the notification of its commencement. This benchmark is based on:

an assumption of cooperation
purposeful management by you, and
the use of our formal powers where there is a genuine lack of cooperation.

 

 

	

Note: 80% of all PGE compliance work should be completed within the stated cycle times as advised in the confirmation letter, at the audit commencement interview and in the audit management plan.

What we expect from Tax officers and Large Market taxpayers is detailed more fully in the Large business and tax compliance.

Taxpayer expectations of the ATO

The taxpayer can expect the compliance officer to:

complete a case in the shortest possible time practicable, with minimum inconvenience and disruption to the taxpayer
act in a professional, courteous and respectful manner and demonstrate integrity, fairness and impartiality in the conduct of their duties;
maintain open and frank dialogue, including informing you regularly of the progress of the compliance activity
advise you of any technical issues that are to be escalated to ATO experts, allow for your views on the technical issues to be provided to the ATO technical decision maker and facilitate access to the ATO technical decision maker to discuss your views on the technical issues if required
ensure information requests are clear and unambiguous; and
recognise your right to have advisers present during discussions and meetings and to allow you to confer with them as necessary.

ATO expectation of the taxpayer

The ATO expects the taxpayer can assist in the efficient and timely progress of the compliance activity by:

engaging with us in developing realistic plans, milestones and timeframes
advising us at the earliest opportunity of any issues that may result in timeframes or milestones being delayed, including problems with access to personnel or information
provide us with access to the key personnel in your organisation who can readily provide the financial and business information required
engaging in open and frank dialogue on the taxation issues raised by the audit
taking all reasonable steps to comply with our requests for information and ensure all relevant recording systems (for example, email, business reporting, directors minutes and board papers) are searched and disclosed where appropriate
explaining the basis of any claim you make for legal professional privilege or confidentiality
advise us at the earliest opportunity if you consider the compliance activity is not tracking to plan
ensuring your public officer and tax function are adequately supported and resourced to manage the audit and provide information and responses to us in a timely manner
ensuring documentation is readily accessible (physical document or electronic)
providing information to help us understand the business context as this is central to identifying and evaluating tax risks, and
escalating any concerns you have about the audit or the conduct of our officers to senior tax officers.
Escalation

The escalation process should be discussed at the initial interview and include the identification of the relevant officers to whom the matter is to be escalated within both organisations.

The desire to maintain a cooperative relationship should not impede raising matters of concern during the course of the review or audit. The nature of a cooperative approach requires both parties to work together to resolve issues as they arise. Failure to do so can lead to greater longer term damage to the relationship and quality outcomes.

Early intervention is critical in avoiding ongoing problems. Often there are different perceptions as to the cause and existence of delays which need to be fully discussed if resolution is to be attained. It is expected in the first instance that a discussion should take place between the audit team and the taxpayer s nominated contact to identify and resolve any issues.

Besides exploring the current issues, the discussion should canvass future action should resolution not be reached.

The aim of the escalation process is the timely resolution of any issues regarding the provision of information without resorting to the use of formal powers while maintaining our relationship with the taxpayer. The role of a senior tax officer is not to assume control of the audit, this remains with the audit team, but rather to work with the parties to resolve the issues.

Independent review

 

 

	

When we issue a statement of audit position to a large business taxpayer, the taxpayer may request an independent review of our statement of audit position.

When areas of disagreement remain after the taxpayer has considered the statement of audit position, the taxpayer may request an independent review of areas of disagreement within 10 working days of the date of the statement of audit position. This review will be conducted by a senior officer from Objections and Review.

