Activity statement processing manual

Instructions on processing activity statements

Last published 16 July 2026

Favourite Notes 

All revisions to lodged activity statement amounts and amounts for ATO original (default) assessments must be input in IPS.

Automated notices of assessment/amended assessment covering all tax periods issue from the Digital Communication Services (DCS). To reissue a DCS automated notice to a taxpayer, follow the reissue outbound correspondence procedure.

A notice of assessment/amended assessment will issue automatically unless it is specifically suppressed (for example it is necessary to personally serve a manual notice).

Prior to 20 June 2020, activity statement notices were processed in ADD which is now a read-only system. You can access ADD to view and print notices that issued prior to this system change. To re-issue a copy of the ADD notice to a taxpayer, follow Task 15 of the COTD main process
Opens in a new window
.

BEFORE closing your case

You must:

Correct any IPS processing errors
Opens in a new window
 (for example exception, BT).

Ensure that the activity statement amounts (for example amounts reported on the lodged activity statement, self and ATO revisions) are posted to the taxpayer's Integrated Client Account (ICA) in ICP

Ensure any activity statement refund issues. Where the refund has not issued, complete all the checks in the Review items, suppressions and accounting treatments procedure
Opens in a new window
. If required, escalate to Accounting via a Siebel activity; refer to Escalating activity statement refunds.

Where you have taken all relevant corrective steps, but the status of the activity statement has not moved from 'RCVD' to 'FIN', complete the FBI RLI Front Door
Opens in a new window
Opens in a new window
 request form 
Opens in a new window
(select Work requests at the 'Request Type' dropdown list). This form is actioned by Frontline Services (FS) who will resolve the issue. If required, FS will raise an incident. 
Actions to be completed in IPS

You are required to complete a range of functions in IPS as a result of a compliance activity or objection. These actions include:

generating or reissuing activity statements

keying SLF original activity statements (processing lodgments)

revising activity statements (SLF and ATO)

processing ATO original assessments

dealing with held activity statements

identifying and actioning/escalating IPS processing errors appropriately

terminating or cancelling activity statements

issuing or suppressing system generated notices

completion of activity statement notes

providing copies of activity statements.

BEFORE inputting amounts in IPS
Check the postal address status in ICP

An Unreliable address details indicator will display:

on the top right-hand corner at the 'Client home' screen.
under Contact details under 'Client menu', under the Addresses tab.
Check the Reliability column for 'Unreliable' or 'Reliable' status or end date. Where the postal address is 'Unreliable', the automated notice will not issue.

Where there is no reliable address for the ICA, you will need to conduct searches to locate a current postal address and then update the address in ICP
Opens in a new window
. The automated notice will then issue to this address.

Check for active suppressions in ICP

If a 'suppress all', 'suppress debit processing', 'suppress credit processing', 'suppress general correspondence' or 'lock down suppression' indicator exists on the taxpayer's ICA in ICP:

the automated notice of assessment/amended assessment will not issue
the suppression needs to be end-dated. If you did not place the suppression, you need to contact the relevant officer.
the suppression can be end-dated with SAR R0133 Modify suppressions or by following the Review items, suppressions and accounting treatments procedure
Opens in a new window
.

Once the automated notice has issued, consider if the suppression needs to be applied again.

A suppress debit processing suppression will stop a debit notice of assessment from issuing and has no impact on a credit notice of assessment. A suppress credit processing suppression will stop a credit notice of assessment from issuing and has no impact on a debit notice of assessment. For more information on suppressions, refer to the Suppressions - overview, list and view
Opens in a new window
.
Currency of registration information and registration updates made in ICP

When registration updates are made in ICP the changes are not automatically reflected in IPS. The corrective action required in IPS depends on the period of the registration updated and whether activity statements relating to those periods have already issued.

Where registration changes relate to periods where the activity statement has:

Not issued

	
and is not past the due date for issue, the system will, at due date for issue, generate an activity statement that includes the new obligations resulting from the update.

and is past the due date for issue, manual generation of the activity statement is required (refer to generate activity statement). The newly generated activity statement should be checked to ensure that the correct obligations are reflected.




Issued

	

There is no automatic updating or reissuing of the activity statement in IPS to reflect the registration changes.

If you are revising these periods the revised activity statement (new activity statement generated) will reflect the new obligations.

If you are not revising these periods, you must terminate/cancel the original and reissue a new activity statement with the new obligations reflected on it. In these circumstances you will also need to decide whether the new activity statement is:

issued to the taxpayer for lodgment, or

retained in the office and keyed as an original assessment. 

For details on making registration updates refer to the IT, GST and Excise Referrals and Escalations Guide.

Implications of the self-actuating & self-assessment systems and backdating GST registration

Under the self-actuating system (tax periods starting before 1 July 2012), a taxpayer's GST registration:

May be backdated from when they were required to be registered.
Note: a taxpayer is required to be registered for GST if carrying on an enterprise and annual turnover meets the registration turnover threshold.
Where backdated beyond four years, it generally means we can only recover an unpaid amount (and correspondingly the taxpayer is only entitled to a refund or credit) within the four-year time limit. Therefore, our ability to backdate a taxpayer's GST registration should be limited to four years.

Under the self-assessment system (tax periods starting on or after 1 July 2012), in respect of a taxpayer's GST registration:

Our ability to backdate is specifically limited to four years.
Where there is a determination of fraud or evasion, a taxpayer's GST registration may be backdated beyond four years.

 

GST reporting methods (BAS post 1 July 2017)
Option 1 - Full reporting - calculate and report GST quarterly.
Option 2 - Simpler BAS
Option 3 - Instalment reporting - pay GST instalment amount and lodge an annual GST return.
Option 1 - Full reporting

This option requires the taxpayer to calculate and report GST quarterly as follows:

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1B
Label G1	Label G10
Label G2	Label G11
Label G3	


Where the compliance activity detects that a revision is required to any of the above labels, you should revise the relevant labels on the activity statement for the particular tax periods.

Example 1

	

The taxpayer disclosed an amount of $50,000 at G2 (exports). As a result of conducting an audit, the amount should be $20,000. Therefore, you should input $20,000 at G2.




Example 2

	

The taxpayer disclosed an amount of $70,000 at G11 (non-capital purchases). As a result of conducting an audit, the amount should be $59,000 (overstated by $11,000). Therefore, you should input $59,000 at G11.

Option 2 - Simpler BAS

This option requires the taxpayer to calculate and report GST across all cycles

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1 B
Label G1	

Where the compliance activity detects a revision is required to any of the above labels, you should revise the relevant labels on the relevant activity statement.

Streamlined taxpayers for BAS periods pre-1 July 2017 are only required to report actual GST at labels G1, 1A and 1B. From 1 July 2017, any outstanding Annual GST Information Reports for the 2016-17 FY and earlier will no longer be required. 

 

Option 3 - GST instalment reporting

This option requires the taxpayer to:

pay a GST instalment (either calculated by the ATO or varied by the taxpayer), and
lodge an annual GST return.

The annual GST return will provide:

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1 B
Label G1	Label G10
Label G2	Label G11
Label G3	

 

Any revisions should be made to the relevant GST label on the annual GST return. The annual GST return is used to account for any difference between the actual GST liability and the total GST instalments for the year.

Where the annual return has not been lodged (that is, is not yet due) you cannot revise the activity statement however you should advise the taxpayer to include the revised amount in the annual GST return when lodged.

Revisions cannot be made to the GST instalment amount on the quarterly activity statement.

Where an entity is a GST instalment payer, and has either not lodged a GST annual return, or has lodged one but has understated its liability for the period, any revision or assessment of that liability is also an assessment of net amount for the purposes of Schedule 1 to the TAA.

As a general rule, you would not raise such an assessment for a GST instalment payer until after the due date for the lodgment of the GST annual return. The only exception to this rule would be if there was a threat to the revenue by not raising a liability early, for example, if the entity was about to leave the country and consideration was being given to issue a Departure Prohibition Order (DPO).

The Commissioner may make an assessment at any time, but it would not generally be done before the liability due date unless there are special circumstances of the type described above. If at any time you believe there are such special circumstances, obtain the advice of your team leader and EL2 branch manager before you proceed with any assessment.

Further, where you consider raising an assessment before the end of the tax period, you should consider the policy outlined in Making an assessment before a tax period has ended
