The GST reporting option selected by taxpayer must be considered (BAS pre 1 July 2017)

Before revising an activity statement in IPS, you need to be aware of the GST reporting option the taxpayer is using. The options are:

Option 1 - Full reporting - calculate and report GST quarterly.

Option 2 - Streamlined reporting - calculate GST quarterly.

Option 3 - Instalment reporting - pay GST instalment amount and lodge an annual GST return.

Option 1 - Full reporting

This option requires the taxpayer to calculate and report GST quarterly as follows:

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1B
Label G1	Label G10
Label G2	Label G11
Label G3	

Where the compliance activity detects that a revision is required to any of the above labels, you should revise the relevant labels on the activity statement for the particular tax periods.

Example 1

	

The taxpayer disclosed an amount of $50,000 at G2 (exports). As a result of conducting an audit, the amount should be $20,000. Therefore, you should input $20,000 at G2.




Example 2

	

The taxpayer disclosed an amount of $70,000 at G11 (non-capital purchases). As a result of conducting an audit, the amount should be $59,000 (overstated by $11,000). Therefore, you should input $59,000 at G11.

Option 2 - Streamlined reporting

This option requires the taxpayer to calculate and report GST quarterly.

The quarterly activity statement requires the following:

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1B
Label G1	

Where the compliance activity detects a revision is required to any of the above labels, you should revise the relevant labels on the relevant quarterly activity statement.

Streamlined taxpayers for BAS periods pre-1 July 2017 are only required to report actual GST at labels G1, 1A and 1B. From 1 July 2017, any outstanding Annual GST Information Reports for the 2016-17 FY and earlier will no longer be required. 
Option 3 - GST instalment

This option requires the taxpayer to:

pay a GST instalment (either calculated by the ATO or varied by the taxpayer), and
lodge an annual GST return.

The annual GST return will provide:

Sales	Purchases
Label 1A	Label 1B
Label G1	Label G10
Label G2	Label G11
Label G3	

Any revisions should be made to the relevant GST label on the annual GST return. The annual GST return is used to account for any difference between the actual GST liability and the total GST instalments for the year.

Where the annual return has not been lodged (that is, is not yet due) you cannot revise the activity statement however you should advise the taxpayer to include the revised amount in the annual GST return when lodged.

 Revisions cannot be made to the GST instalment amount on the quarterly activity statement.
	

Taxpayer variations to the ATO calculated GST instalment amount

You cannot vary a taxpayer's variation of the ATO calculated instalment amount. Any revisions should be made to the relevant GST label on the annual GST return.

PAYG instalments payment options

There are 2 PAYG instalment payment options available. These are:

Option 1 - Pay a PAYG instalment amount.

Option 2 - Calculate PAYG instalment using the actual income multiplied by a rate.

For further guidance on when and how to make revisions relating to PAYG instalments refer to the PAYG instalment method
Opens in a new window
. 

Option 1 - Pay a PAYG instalment amount

This option is available to:

all individuals
companies and superannuation funds with business or investment income of $2m or less in their most recently assessed income tax return, and
companies and superannuation funds eligible to pay quarterly PAYG instalments.

The instalment amount is calculated by using the business and investment income from the most recently assessed income tax return.

Option 2 - Calculate PAYG instalment

This option is available to all taxpayers.
The instalment amount is based on the taxpayer's actual income multiplied by a rate which is provided by the ATO.

Variation of the instalment rate by taxpayers

The taxpayer may vary the ATO instalment rate and their earlier instalment rate if they:

think the amount advised by the ATO will result in them paying more (or less) than the expected tax for the income year, and
choose to estimate their investment and business income and the tax on the income.
