Where you do have access to cancel activity statements, you must cancel the activity statements for the particular tax period in the correct sequence in IPS, that is, from the most recently lodged activity statement to the oldest activity statement.

If the activity statements are cancelled out of sequence, the tax period is 'broken' in ICP. This means that the amount for the cancelled activity statement will not post to ICP and further rekeying or cancelling will not post the amount. A data fix will then need to be lodged.

Example:

For the July-Sept 2021 quarterly period, the original activity statement (SLF ORG) and 3 self-revisions (SLF REV) are lodged and finalised in IPS (total of 4 activity statements). The taxpayer's identity has been compromised and so it is decided to cancel the 4 activity statements for this tax period.

The activity statements must be cancelled in the following order:

Cancel the third self-revision (most recently lodged)
Cancel the second self-revision
Cancel the first self-revision
Cancel the self-original (oldest).

Where there are activity statements for more than one period that need to be cancelled, they do not need to be cancelled in a particular sequence based on the period.

Assessments and amended assessments

Where the ATO makes a determination of a taxpayer's net amount and/or net fuel amount for a tax period, an assessment is made. An amended assessment occurs when the original assessment amount is revised.

An assessment/amended assessment can only be made of the net amount (GST, LCT, WET) and net fuel amount (fuel tax credits amounts) on an activity statement. 

Where other labels are changed (for example, PAYGW, PAYGI or FBT) they do not form part of the assessment.

When you make or amend an assessment it is vital that the taxpayer receives a notice of assessment or a notice of amended assessment. This is a formal notice advising the taxpayer of their new assessed/amended net amount and/or new assessed/amended net fuel amount. An automated notice will issue covering all tax periods. Where you are required to issue a manual notice, see Manual notices.

