Self-assessment system

Self-assessment for amounts under indirect tax laws (that is, GST, LCT, WET and fuel tax) was introduced on 1 July 2012 and applies to tax periods starting on or after 1 July 2012.

When a taxpayer lodges an activity statement, the Commissioner is treated as having assessed the net amount and/or net fuel amount on lodgment. The activity statement will be treated as the notice of assessment and no actual notice of assessment will issue.

Where an amount on an activity statement is amended by the ATO (for example, as a result of compliance action), a notice of amended assessment will issue. This is because an assessment is made on lodgment of the activity statement and if this assessment is then later amended, a notice of amended assessment will issue.

Example:

As a result of an audit, you examine the taxpayer's activity statement for the December 2018 tax period. You make an amended assessment of the taxpayer's net amount for this tax period. You revise the activity statement in IPS and an automated notice of amended assessment issues.

Where the taxpayer makes a revision to their activity statement, this is a self-revision. Where the self-revision is via an RBAS (paper or via online channels) and it is made within the period of review and no changes are made to the amounts on the self-revision, the RBAS is taken to be the notice of amended assessment, and an actual notice of amended assessment does not issue. However, a notice of amended assessment will issue where:

a self-revision is lodged in writing (for example, letter)
a self-revision is made during the period of review, but the ATO does not fully accept the amounts and amends only in part
the taxpayer applies for the amendment during for the period of review and we make the revision after the period of review.

Where the taxpayer has failed to lodge an activity statement and we make an assessment of the net amount and/or net fuel amount, a notice of assessment will issue.

Self-actuating system

The self-actuating system will continue to apply to tax periods starting before 1 July 2012. When a taxpayer lodges an original activity statement, no assessment is taken to have been made at the time of lodgment.

To give effect to a revision, the Commissioner must assess the net amount and/or net fuel amount for a tax period. The revision will not be legally valid unless a notice has issued. This applies to both ATO-initiated and taxpayer-initiated activity statement revisions (including where a taxpayer makes a self-revision request during compliance activity).

Where there has been no previous revision, you must issue a notice of assessment to give effect to the revision. Where there has been a previous revision, a notice of amended assessment must issue.

Example - ATO-initiated revision:

As a result of an audit, you assess the net amount for the September 2011 tax period. You revise the activity statement in IPS and a notice of assessment issues. The taxpayer objects and the Objections and Review officer allow the objection. The subsequent assessment completed by this officer for the September 2011 tax period is an amended assessment, and a notice of amended assessment will issue. 

