lodging a self-revision electronically (for example, via online services)
lodging a paper revised activity statement (paper RBAS) or
making a request via letter (this can be sent to the ATO or provided to you when conducting the compliance activity).

Where the taxpayer lodges the self-revision during compliance activity via a revised activity statement (electronic or paper), the amounts are automatically input in IPS. Where the taxpayer lodges the self-revision via letter provided to you, you are required to input the amounts as a self-revision in IPS.

The taxpayer's amounts on the self-revision are accepted in full, accepted in part or not accepted. On 1 July 2012, system changes were made to IPS with the introduction of self-assessment. The reason for these system changes is that a self-revision is treated differently under the self-actuating system (tax periods starting before 1 July 2012) and self-assessment system (tax periods starting on or after 1 July 2012).

Under the self-actuating system, where a taxpayer lodges a self-revision and the amounts are accepted, an assessment/amended assessment must be issued. Where the amounts provided on the self-revision are amended (as the amounts are accepted in part or not accepted), an assessment/amended assessment must be issued for the ATO revision.

Note: a notice of assessment or notice of amended assessment must issue to ensure revisions for tax periods prior to 1 July 2012 are legally valid. It is not sufficient for the revised amount to only be posted to the taxpayer's ICA. This applies to both ATO-initiated and taxpayer-initiated revisions (including where a taxpayer makes a self-revision request during compliance activity that we accept in full, in part or do not accept). See Pre-1 July 2012 activity statement revisions.

Under self-assessment, where a taxpayer lodges a self-revision via a paper RBAS or via online services, it is merely an amendment application, and the Commissioner may or may not amend the taxpayer's assessment to give effect to the amendment application. Where the Commissioner gives effect to the amendment application (that is, amounts provided by the taxpayer are accepted in full), an amended assessment is made when the amounts are posted to the taxpayer's ICA in ICP. In these cases, the amendment application (that is, revised activity statement) is taken to be the notice of amended assessment and as such, an actual notice of amended assessment will not issue.

During compliance activity:

Where a taxpayer makes a self-revision during compliance activity you must consider the case circumstances to determine how the self-revision must be treated. In making this determination you must consider:

the amount of the revisions
the reason for the revisions
whether the revisions are within scope of the audit/review
whether the amounts of the revisions will be accepted or not, and
whether the revisions could represent an attempt to claw back amounts previously disallowed.

Self-revisions by a taxpayer are actioned differently in IPS depending on:

the lodgment channel used by the taxpayer to lodge the self-revision
the particular tax period
the outcome of the compliance activity (that is, the taxpayer's amounts are accepted, accepted in part or not accepted), and
whether or not the self-revision is in 'received' or 'finalised' status.

