Self-revisions and shortfall penalty

Generally, self-revisions lodged by the taxpayer before the notification of an audit or review have no administrative penalty applied.

However, depending on the circumstances of each case, self-revisions made by the taxpayer once compliance activity has commenced may not be treated in the same way.

Where an activity statement is under review/audit and is subsequently self-revised by the taxpayer, you must:

determine whether a false and misleading statement in a material particular was made (refer to PS LA 2012/5
Opens in a new window
)

determine whether a shortfall has been made
calculate the shortfall amount by taking into account:

For the purpose of calculating the shortfall, the taxpayer's amounts as per the self-revision should be viewed from the relevant DIN (SLF REV) in the 'Activity Statement List' in IPS and not from the 'Taxpayer Provided Values' window. Do not view the ICA in ICP as the amounts for the SLF REV will not be posted where the SLF REV is not accepted.

consider how the shortfall will be treated - the timing of the self-revision (that is, if made before or after-tax audit notification) will determine if administrative penalty will apply.

The final treatment will depend on the case circumstances and should be discussed and decided in close consultation with your team leader. For further information on voluntary disclosures, refer to Miscellaneous Taxation Ruling MT 2012/3 Administrative penalties: voluntary disclosures
Opens in a new window
.

The table below provides some examples of self-revisions made during compliance activity and how they should be treated.

Example 1

	

Self-revision is made prior to you identifying/quantifying and communicating the errors or omissions - it may be appropriate to treat the self-revision as a voluntary disclosure prior to audit.




Example 2

	

Self-revision is made after you have identified errors but prior to quantifying the full amounts or communicating your decision to the taxpayer - it may be appropriate to treat the revision as a voluntary disclosure made after commencement of an audit.




Example 3

	

Self-revision is made after you have identified/quantified and communicated your decision to the taxpayer - it may be appropriate to assess the shortfall in relation to penalties for false and misleading statement as if the self-revision had not been made.

Post compliance activity

A taxpayer may make self-revisions to activity statements after compliance activity has been completed.

ALL self-revisions are risk rated.

Where the self-revision needs to be verified, you need to determine if the self-revision is:

legitimate, for example, the taxpayer is correcting an error they have found such as one relating to an issue that was not considered as part of the audit, or
represents an attempt to claw back errors/omissions detected and revised as part of the previously completed compliance activity.

As part of this process officers from other areas may contact you to discuss the case.

Where the circumstances behind the self-revision indicate potential fraud, you should consider the Fraud & Criminal Behaviours referral guidelines
Opens in a new window
.

Where the circumstances behind the self-revision indicate a lack of understanding of the taxpayer's review/objection rights, the self-revision should be reversed by an amended assessment (processing an ATO revision), and the taxpayer should be encouraged to exercise their rights under the appropriate review process.

